1 OCCU Comte de la Marche et de Ponthieu 1342-1362, comte de Ponthieu 1351-1360, connétable de France 1354-1356, Count of La Marche, Greve i  Marche och i Ponthieu, count de la marche
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT James I of Bourbon (1319 – 6 April 1362), was a French prince du sang, and the son of Louis I, Duke of Bourbon and Mary of Avesnes. He was Count of Ponthieu from 1351 to 1360, and Count of La Marche from 1341 to his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_I,_Count_of_La_Marche#:~:text=James%20I%20of%20Bourbon%20(1319,from%201341%20to%20his%20death
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00002108&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacques 1er de Bourbon, né en 1319, mort à Lyon en 1362, fut comte de la Marche de 1342 à 1362, comte de Ponthieu de 1351 à 1360 et connétable de France de 1354 à 1356.
2 CONT
2 CONT Il était fils de Louis Ier, duc de Bourbon et comte de la Marche, et de Marie d'Avesnes. Il est ancêtre par les mâles du roi Henri IV.
2 CONT
2 CONT Il combattit en 1341 et 1342 pour le compte de Charles de Blois contre Jean de Montfort qui luttaient pour la succession de Bretagne. En 1351, le roi Jean II le Bon lui donne le Ponthieu
2 CONT
2 CONT Il est ensuite présent à la bataille de Crécy (1346), où il est blessé. À Poitiers (1356), il est également blessé et fait prisonnier. La paix de Brétigny signée (1360), il perd le Ponthieu, rendu aux Anglais, et combat pour débar
2 CONC rasser le royaume des bandes de routiers, ces mercenaires démobilisés qui se mettent à piller les campagnes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Un engagement eut lieu à Brignais, près de Lyon, où le comte de la Marche et son fils aîné furent mortellement blessés.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Il avait épousé en 1335 Jeanne de Châtillon, dame de Condé et de Carency (1320 † 1371), et avait eu :
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabelle (1340 † 1371), mariée en premières noces en 1362 avec Louis II de Brienne, vicomte de Beaumont († 1364), puis en secondes noces en 1364 avec Bouchard VII († 1371), comte de Vendôme et de Castres 
2 CONT
2 CONT Pierre (1342 † 1362), comte de la Marche 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean Ier (1344 † 1393), comte de la Marche et de Vendôme 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacques Ier (1346 † 1417), seigneur de Préaux, tige de la branche des Bourbons seigneurs de Préaux. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Précédé par Jacques Ier de Bourbon-La Marche Suivi par 
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis Ier 
2 CONT
2 CONT comte de La Marche Pierre 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_I,_Count_of_La_Marche
2 CONT
2 CONT James of Bourbon-La Marche (1319 – April 6, 1362) was the son of Louis I, Duke of Bourbon and Mary of Avesnes. He was Count of Ponthieu from 1351 to 1360, and Count of La Marche from 1356 to his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1335, he married Jeanne of Chatillon, daughter of Hugh of Chatillon, Lord of Leuze.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabelle (1340–1371), married Louis II, Viscount of Beaumont-au-Maine, in Lyon (1362); married Bouchard VII, Count of Vendôme (1364); 
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter II, Count of La Marche (1342–1362); 
2 CONT
2 CONT John I, Count of La Marche (1344–1393); 
2 CONT
2 CONT James of Bourbon-La Marche, Baron of Argies (1346–1417), married (c. 1385) Marguerite, Lady of Preaux and had issue. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He took part in several campaigns of the Hundred Years War.
2 CONT
2 CONT In June 1347 he commanded an army on the Flemish border together with the Marshal Robert de Waurin. They marched to Béthune, the chief city of north-eastern Artois, which was still in French hands, though the countryside had been 
2 CONC overrun by the Flemish. There they gathered together most of the French border forces including the Béthune garrison, Charles de Montmorency from the sector around Lille and Charles de la Cerda with most of the men from Aire and S
2 CONC aint-Omer. On 13 June they attacked the Flemish camp at night. However the Flemings managed to regroup and launch a counter-attack before slipping across the border.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1349, he was created Captain-General of Languedoc. Following the fall of Calais in 1347 a truce had been concluded, but in 1349 open warfare broke out again, the most conspicuous event of that year being Henry of Grosmont, 1st 
2 CONC Duke of Lancaster's raid deep into Languedoc to the walls of Toulouse. In early 1350 Jaques was given command of an army mustering at Moissac on the borders of Agenais. This he did 22 February. There, he almost immediately entered
2 CONC  into negotiations with Lancaster with two papal legates acting as mediators. The result was a truce, at first limited to Languedoc and the other provinces where James was Lieutenant, but in April it was extended to the rest of Fr
2 CONC ance.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1354 he was appointed Constable of France. In January and February 1355 as Constable, he took part in planning the resupmtion of the war with England. However the war soon became a matter of secondary importance as the French g
2 CONC overnment became embroiled in the intrigues of Charles II of Navarre. In May 1355 it became apparent that open war was about to begin between the King of France and a King of Navarre allied to England. James belonged to the party 
2 CONC fronted by the Dowager Queens, Jeanne d'Évreux and Blance d'Évreux, who lobbied John II on Charles of Navarre's behalf. In the end, John II gave way and on 31 May agreed to pardon Charles of Navarre.
2 CONT
2 CONT However by the time John II's letters reached Pamplona, the capital of Navarre, Charles and his army had already embarked with a course for the Cotentin Peninsula in Normandy. When the news reached Paris on 4 June it therefore bec
2 CONC ame necessary to prepare the defences of Normandy. Two armies were formed. The largest, of which the Constable was given command, was to be stationed at Caen. He was also appointed one of three conciliators who were to meet with C
2 CONC harles of Navarre as soon as he landed and explain the king's new position. Charles of Navarre arrived at Cherbourg 5 July and the negotiations opened soon after. The result was the Treaty of Valognes sealed on 10 September. Inclu
2 CONC ded among the provisions of the treaty was that seven of Charles' walled towns and castles in Normandy should be nominally surrendered to the Constable.
2 CONT
2 CONT In autumn 1355 the Constable was in the south where he, together with John of Armagnac, who commanded an army of local troops, and the Marshal Jean de Clermont were to defend against the expected invasion of the Edward, Prince of 
2 CONC Wales. When the Prince of Wales struck in October it was further south than expected, in the County of Armagnac, rather than the Garonne valley. The three French commanders hurried south to Toulouse, where they prepared themselves
2 CONC  for a siege. On 28 October the Prince crossed the Garonne and the Ariège, at places never before forded by horses, and marched north to within a few miles of Toulouse. Thinking the English might attempt to invest the city from bo
2 CONC th sides, the Constable left for Montauban to hold the crossings of the Tarn and the Garonne. Instead, the Prince continued eastward into lands previously untouched by the war and largely undefended. On 8 November he took Narbonne
2 CONC , but was now far away from home territory. The Constable and Armagnac followed him east and taking up camp at the town of Homps on the Aude River seem first to have intended to cut off retreat and force him to battle on their own
2 CONC  terms. In the end, no battle took place, as the French commanders decided to withdraw west towards Toulouse.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the English return westward serious disagreements over strategy broke out in Toulouse. During the whole campaign Armagnac had insisted on avoiding battle at all cost, concentrating on defending the principal cities and rive
2 CONC r crossings, thus abandoning the countryside to the English. This was deeply frustrating to the people of Languedoc and also resented by James who longed for action. The last opportunity to stop the English was made at the river S
2 CONC ave, but after watching the enemy for several days Armagnac on 21 November broke the bridges across the river and withdrew north. The Prince of Wales crossed the river the next day and on 28 November was back in English held terri
2 CONC tory. The French conduct of the defence was deeply unpopular in the south and although the Constable had not agreed to Armagnac's strategy and gave the King his own eye-witness account he could not escape the blame directed agains
2 CONC t the three commanders.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 12 November 1355, the day following Edward III of England's return to Calais, after a largely fruitless raid into Picardy, James and the Marshal Arnoul d'Audrehem were behind an abortive proposal to settle the war in single com
2 CONC bat between the two kings.
2 CONT
2 CONT Weary of political intrigues, he resigned the constableship in May of 1356, but fought at the Battle of Poitiers that year where was taken prisoner by the English. His ransom was sold by Jean III de Grailly, captal de Buch and fiv
2 CONC e of his companions for 25 000 écus to the Prince of Wales. James was released by the Treaty of Brétigny. The same treaty surrendered Ponthieu to the English; but he had succeeded his brother Peter in La Marche after the latter's 
2 CONC death at Poitiers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Moreover, the peace promised by the treaty proved illusory. While the English were at peace with France, the discharged mercenary companies found new employment by ravaging the countryside and holding whole cities to ransom. Soon 
2 CONC after his return from captivity, King John II commissioned James and Jean de Tancarville to raise an army to put down the "Free Companies" under the informal leadership of Petit Meschin before they could overrun Burgundy. Bourbon 
2 CONC and Tancarville gathered their army at Brignais. Never dreaming that the companies would dare challenge them in the open they took few steps to secure their camp and when the companies attacked that morning of 6 April 1362 they we
2 CONC re taken completely by surprise. In the battle that followed the governmental army was routed and James and his oldest son were mortally wounded.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Bourbon-111 WikiTree Genealogy] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Feb 23 2021, 3:24:41 UTC''
