1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Jadwiga (Polish) / Hedwig (German) / Hedvig (Hungarian)=
2 CONT Capetian / Anjou of Hungary
2 CONT =Queen of Poland, Grand Duchess of Lithuania=  
2 CONT
2 CONT Born between 3 October 1373 and 18 February 1374, died on 17 July 1399 after childbirth.
2 CONT
2 CONT Named "Hedwig" after her fifth-great-grandmother: [https://www.geni.com/people/St-Hedwig-von-Andechs-Patron-Saint-of-Silesia/4774053172420033191 St. Hedwig of Silesia].
2 CONT
2 CONT =King (Queen) of Poland=
2 CONT * Reign: 16 October 1384 – 17 July 1399
2 CONT * Coronation: 16 October 1384 in the Wawel Cathedral, Kraków
2 CONT * Predecessor: Louis I
2 CONT * Successor: Władysław II Jagiełło 
2 CONT
2 CONT * Spouse: Jogaila
2 CONT * Issue: Elizabeth Bonifacia (died at birth)
2 CONT * House: House of Anjou
2 CONT * Father: Louis I of Hungary
2 CONT * Mother: Elizabeth of Bosnia
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jadwiga_of_Poland Jadwiga of Anjou] (1373/4 – July 17, 1399) was Queen of Poland from 1384 to her death. She was a member of the Capetian House of Anjou and the daughter of King Louis I of Hungary and
2 CONC  Elisabeth of Bosnia. She is known in Polish as Jadwiga, in English and German as Hedwig, in Lithuanian as Jadvyga, in Hungarian as Hedvig, and in Latin as Hedvigis.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Roman Catholic Church venerates her as Saint Hedwig. Jadwiga is the patron saint of queens and of United Europe.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Royal titles==
2 CONT
2 CONT Royal titles in Latin: Hedvigis dei gracia Regina Polonie, necnon terrarum Cracovie, Sandomirie, Syradie, Lancicie, Cuyavie, Pomeranieque domina et heres.
2 CONT
2 CONT English translation: Jadwiga, by the grace of God, Queen of Poland, lady and inheritor of the land of Kraków (Cracow), Sandomierz, Sieradz, Łęczyca, Kuyavia, Pomerania (Pomerelia).
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Life==
2 CONT
2 CONT Jadwiga was the youngest daughter of Louis I of Hungary and of Elizabeth of Bosnia. Jadwiga could claim descent from the House of Piast, the ancient native Polish dynasty on both her mother's and her father's side. Her paternal gr
2 CONC andmother Elisabeth of Cuyavia was the daughter of King Władysław I the Elbow-high, who had reunited Poland in 1320.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jadwiga was brought up at the royal court in Buda and Visegrád, Hungary. In 1378, she was betrothed (sponsalia de futuro) to Habsburg scion William of Austria, and spent about a year at the imperial court in Vienna, Austria. Jadwi
2 CONC ga's father Louis had, in 1364 in Kraków, during festivities known as the Days of Kraków, also made an arrangement with his former father-in-law, Holy Roman Emperor Charles IV, to intermarry their future children: Charles' son and
2 CONC  future Holy Roman Emperor Sigismund of Luxemburg was engaged and married, as a child, to Louis' daughter and future Queen Mary. One of Louis' original plans was to leave the kingdom of Poland to Mary, whose marriage to Sigismund 
2 CONC was more relevant to this end, as Sigismund was an heir in his own right to Poland and was intended to inherit Brandenburg, which was nearer to Poland than to Hungary. Jadwiga's destiny as Austrian consort was a better fit for Hun
2 CONC gary, as it was an immediate neighbor of Austria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jadwiga was well-educated and a polyglot, speaking Latin, Bosnian, Hungarian, Serbian, Polish, and German, and was interested in the arts, music, science, and court life. She was also known for her piety and her admiration for Sai
2 CONC nts Mary, Martha, and Bridget of Sweden, as well as her patron saint, Hedwig of Andechs.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Reign==
2 CONT
2 CONT Until 1370, Poland had been ruled by the native Piast Dynasty. Its last king, Casimir III, had left no legitimate son and considered his male grandchildren either unsuited or too young to reign. He therefore decided that his survi
2 CONC ving sister Elizabeth of Poland and her son, Louis I of Hungary, should succeed him. Louis was proclaimed king, while Elizabeth held much of the practical power until her death in 1380.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Louis died in 1382, his eldest surviving daughter, Mary, inherited the Hungarian throne under the regency of their Bosnian mother. In Poland, however, the lords of Lesser Poland (Poland's virtual rulers) did not want to conti
2 CONC nue the personal union with Hungary or accept Mary's fiancé, Sigismund, as regent, whom they expelled from the country. They therefore chose Mary's younger sister, Jadwiga, as their new monarch. After two years' negotiations with 
2 CONC Jadwiga's mother, Elizabeth of Bosnia, who was regent of Hungary, and a civil war in Greater Poland (1383), Jadwiga finally came to Kraków and, at the age of ten, on November 16, 1384, was crowned King of Poland — Hedvig Rex Polon
2 CONC iæ, not Hedvig Regina Poloniæ, as the Polish law had no provision for a female ruler (queen). The masculine gender of her title also emphasized that she was monarch in her own right, not a queen consort.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a child monarch of Poland, Jadwiga had at least one relative in Poland (all her immediate family having remained in Hungary): her mother's childless uncle, Władysław the White (d. 1388), Prince of Gniewkowo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Soon after Jadwiga's coronation, new suitors for Jadwiga's hand appeared: Duke Siemowit IV of Masovia and Grand Duke Jogaila of Lithuania, the latter supported by the lords of Lesser Poland. In 1385 (when Jadwiga was eleven years 
2 CONC old) William of Austria came to Kraków to consummate the marriage and present the lords with a fait accompli. His plan, however, failed, and William was expelled from Poland while Jadwiga declared her sponsalia invalid. William la
2 CONC ter married Jadwiga's cousin and rival, Joan II of Naples. That same year (1385), Jogaila and the lords of Lesser Poland signed the Union of Krewo whereby Jogaila pledged to adopt Western Christianity and unite Lithuania with Pola
2 CONC nd in exchange for Jadwiga's hand and the Polish crown. Twelve-year-old Jadwiga and 26-year-old Jogaila — who had earlier been baptized Władysław — wed in March 1385 at Kraków. Jogaila was then crowned King of Poland, though Jadwi
2 CONC ga retained her royal rights. In 1386, Jadwiga's mother Elizabeth and her sister Queen Maria of Hungary were kidnapped, probably on the order of Maria's husband and consort Sigismund.
2 CONT
2 CONT In January 1387, Elizabeth was strangled, while Maria was released in July of the same year through the efforts of the future Frankopan family and Jadwiga's adopted maternal uncle, King Tvrtko of Bosnia. Maria, heavily pregnant, d
2 CONC ied in 1395 under suspicious circumstances.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a monarch, young Jadwiga probably had little actual power. Nevertheless, she was actively engaged in her kingdom's political, diplomatic, and cultural life and acted as the guarantor of Władysław's promises to reclaim Poland's 
2 CONC lost territories. In 1387, Jadwiga led two successful military expeditions to reclaim the province of Halych in Red Ruthenia, which Hungary had retained in a dynastic dispute at her accession. As an heiress to Louis I of Hungary, 
2 CONC the expeditions were largely peaceful and resulted in Petru I of Moldavia paying homage to the Polish monarchs in September 1387. In 1390 she began a correspondence with the Teutonic Knights, followed by personal meetings in which
2 CONC  she opened diplomatic negotiations herself.
2 CONT
2 CONT Most political responsibilities, however, likely lay with Władysław, while Jadwiga attended to cultural and charitable activities. She sponsored writers and artists and donated much of her personal wealth, including her royal insi
2 CONC gnia, to charity, for purposes including the founding of hospitals. She financed a scholarship for twenty Lithuanians to study at Charles University in Prague to help strengthen Christianity in their country; for this purpose, she
2 CONC  also founded a bishopric in Vilnius. Among her most notable cultural legacies was the restoration of the Kraków Academy, renamed Jagiellonian University in 1817 in honor of the couple.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Death and inheritance==
2 CONT
2 CONT On June 22, 1399, Jadwiga gave birth to a daughter, Elizabeth Bonifacia. Within a month, both the girl and her mother died from birth complications. They were buried together in Wawel Cathedral. Jadwiga's death undermined Jogaila'
2 CONC s position as King of Poland, but he retained the throne until his death 35 years later.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is not easy to say who Jadwiga's heir was in the line of Poland, or Poland's rightful heir, since Poland had not used primogeniture, and kings had ascended by some sort of election. There were descendants of superseded daughter
2 CONC s of Casimir III of Poland (d. 1370), such as his youngest daughter Anna, Countess of Celje (d. 1425 without surviving issue), and her daughter Anna of Celje (1380–1416), whom Władysław II Jagiełło married next, who had a daughter
2 CONC , Jadwiga of Lithuania, born in 1408. Jadwiga died in 1431, reputedly poisoned by Sophia, Władysław's last wife, after a faction of Polish nobles supported Jadwiga against Sophia's sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT Emperor Sigismund was an heir of Casimir III, as the eldest son of his mother, Elisabeth of Pomerania, who had been the only surviving child of Elisabeth of Poland since 1377, herself daughter of Casimir III from his first marriag
2 CONC e to Gediminaitis Aldona of Lithuania. The family possession of the principality of Kuyavia belonged to Sigismund, who had the strongest hereditary claims. However, the leaders of the country wanted to avoid Sigismund and any pers
2 CONC onal union with Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Other descendants of Władysław the Short (through the Silesian dukes of Świdnica) included the then Emperor Wenceslas, king of Bohemia, who died without issue in 1419, as well as the Silesian dukes of Opole and Sagan.
2 CONT
2 CONT The male-line Piasts were best represented by the Dukes of Masovia, one of whom had sought to marry Jadwiga in 1385. Various princes of Silesia were also of Piast descent, but they had been largely pushed aside since the exile of 
2 CONC Vladislas II, Duke of Kraków.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jadwiga's husband, Władysław Jagiello, kept the throne, mostly because no claimant with clearly better stature appeared. He was never ousted, not even after the death of his second wife, and eventually founded a dynasty in Poland 
2 CONC through the sons of his last wife, who were not related to earlier Polish rulers.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Legends and veneration==
2 CONT
2 CONT From the time of her death, Jadwiga was widely venerated in Poland like a saint, even though she was only beatified in the 1980s and canonized in 1997 by the Polish Pope John Paul II. Numerous miracle legends were recounted to jus
2 CONC tify the desired sainthood. The two best-known are those of "Jadwiga's cross" and "Jadwiga's foot."
2 CONT
2 CONT Jadwiga often prayed before a large black crucifix hanging in the north aisle of Wawel Cathedral. During one of these prayers, the Christ on the cross is said to have spoken to her. The crucifix, "Saint Jadwiga's cross", is still 
2 CONC there, with her relics beneath it.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to another legend, Jadwiga took a piece of jewelry from her foot and gave it to a poor stonemason who had begged for her help. When the King left, he noticed her footprint in the plaster floor of his workplace, even thou
2 CONC gh the plaster had already hardened before her visit. The supposed footprint, known as "Jadwiga's foot", can still be seen in one of Kraków's churches.
2 CONT
2 CONT On June 8, 1979, Pope John Paul II prayed at her sarcophagus, and the Congregation for Divine Worship and the Discipline of the Sacraments officially affirmed her beatification on August 8, 1986. The Pope canonized Jadwiga in Krak
2 CONC ów on June 8, 1997.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Exhumations and sarcophagus==
2 CONT
2 CONT Jadwiga's body has been exhumed at least three times. The first time was in the 17th century, in connection with the construction of a bishop's sarcophagus next to Jadwiga's grave. The next exhumation took place in 1887. They foun
2 CONC d Jadwiga's complete skeleton, along with a mantle and hat. Jan Matejko sketched Jadwiga's skull, which later helped him paint her portrait (see above).
2 CONT
2 CONT On July 12, 1949, they opened her grave again. This time, she was reburied in a sarcophagus paid for by Karol Lanckoroński, sculpted in white marble in 1902 by Antoni Madeyski. The queen is depicted with a dog, a symbol of fidelit
2 CONC y, at her feet. The sarcophagus is oriented with Jadwiga's feet pointing west, unlike all the other sarcophagi in the cathedral. On display next to the sarcophagus are the modest wooden orb and scepter with which the queen had bee
2 CONC n buried - she had sold her jewels to finance the renovation of the Kraków Academy, known today as Jagiellonian University.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==External Linsk & Sources:==
