3 CITY Alcácer do Sal
3 CTRY Portugal
1 OCCU Infante Joao of Portugal, b. 1-13-1399/1400; m. 11-11-1424
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 1.º Senhor da Quinta e morgado da Bacalhôa, Azeitão, Setubal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Constable of the Kingdom and grandfather of Isabella of Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Infante João, 1st Lord of Reguengos de Monsaraz, Colares and Belas (pron. IPA: [ʒu'ɐ̃ũ]; English: John) (Santarém, January 13, 1400 – October 18, 1442, Alcácer do Sal) was a Portuguese infante of the House of Aviz, son of King
2 CONC  John I of Portugal and his wife Philippa of Lancaster.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the 3rd Constable of Portugal, after the death of Nuno Álvares Pereira, and the 10th Master of the Order of St. James. He was also the 1st Lord of Reguengos de Monsaraz, Colares and Belas. In 1424, John married his half-nie
2 CONC ce Isabella of Braganza, daughter of his half-brother Afonso, Duke of Braganza.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the reign of his brother Edward, John joined his brother Pedro, Duke of Coimbra against the expedition to Tangier that would end in a disaster. After the disaster he defended that the recently conquered Ceuta should be offe
2 CONC red in exchange for his brother Ferdinand, the Saint Prince, that was captive in Tangier, something that was against Ferdinand's wishes.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the beginning of his nephew Afonso V's reign, the regency of the kingdom was delegated on Queen Mother Leonor of Aragon (his sister-in-law). This decision in the will of late King Edward was controversial and soon popular disco
2 CONC ntent grew. It was John who installed himself in the capital Lisbon and avoided a rebellion. After that he refused the proposals for an alliance with Leonor of Aragon and his half-brother Afonso, Duke of Braganza, and defended the
2 CONC  summoning of the Cortes to name Pedro, Duke of Coimbra the new regent.
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Diogo of Portugal, Constable of Portugal (1423–1443) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabel of Portugal (1428–1496), married to John II of Castile and mother of Isabella I of Castile and Alfonso, Prince of Asturias 
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatriz of Portugal (1430–1506), married to Infante dom Ferdinand, Duke of Viseu 
2 CONT
2 CONT Filipa of Portugal (1432 – c. 1450), Lady of Almada 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT The Infante João, 1st Lord of Reguengos de Monsaraz, Colares and Belas (pron. IPA: [ʒu'ɐ̃ũ]; English: John) (Santarém, January 13, 1400 – October 18, 1442, Alcácer do Sal) was a Portuguese infante of the House of Aviz, son of King
2 CONC  John I of Portugal and his wife Philippa of Lancaster.
2 CONT
2 CONT Coat of Arms of Infante João, Lord of Reguengos, Belas and Colares.He was the 3rd Constable of Portugal, after the death of Nuno Álvares Pereira, and the 10th Master of the Order of St. James. He was also the 1st Lord of Reguengos
2 CONC  de Monsaraz, Colares and Belas. In 1424, John married his half-niece Isabella of Braganza, daughter of his half-brother Afonso, Duke of Braganza.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the reign of his brother Edward, John joined his brother Pedro, Duke of Coimbra against the expedition to Tangier that would end in a disaster. After the disaster he defended that the recently conquered Ceuta should be offe
2 CONC red in exchange for his brother Ferdinand, the Saint Prince, that was captive in Tangier, something that was against Ferdinand's wishes.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the beginning of his nephew Afonso V's reign, the regency of the kingdom was delegated on Queen Mother Leonor of Aragon (his sister-in-law). This decision in the will of late King Edward was controversial and soon popular disco
2 CONC ntent grew. It was John who installed himself in the capital Lisbon and avoided a rebellion. After that he refused the proposals for an alliance with Leonor of Aragon and his half-brother Afonso, Duke of Braganza, and defended the
2 CONC  summoning of the Cortes to name Pedro, Duke of Coimbra the new regent.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Diogo of Portugal, Constable of Portugal (1423–1443) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabel of Portugal (1428–1496), married to John II of Castile and mother of Isabella I of Castile and Alfonso, Prince of Asturias 
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatriz of Portugal (1430–1506), married to Infante dom Ferdinand, Duke of Viseu 
2 CONT
2 CONT Filipa of Portugal (1432 – c. 1450), Lady of Almada 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
