1 OCCU duc de Brabant, duc de Limbourg (1415-1427)
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''John IV, Duke of Brabant (11 June 1403 – 17 April 1427)[1] was the son''' of Antoine of Burgundy, Duke of Brabant, Lothier and Limburg and his first wife Jeanne of Saint-Pol. '''He was the second Brabantian rule
2 CONC r from the House of Valois. He is best known for founding the University of Louvain (Leuven) in 1425.'''
2 CONT Succession as Duke of Brabant
2 CONT '''John was born in Arras, and succeeded as duke of Brabant in 1415, after his father's death at the Battle of Agincourt. However, his succession was not immediately accepted by everyone. The holy Roman emperor Sigismund contested
2 CONC  his succession, as he wished to curb the Valois influence (read: the influence of the duke of Burgundy, John's uncle) in the region. John the Fearless, the duke of Burgundy, naturally supported his nephew, as did the States of Br
2 CONC abant. Their joint support prevented a second succession war in the duchy.[2] Furthermore, through their support of John as the new duke, the States of Brabant gained regency over the duchy until the coming of age of the still und
2 CONC erage duke.'''[3]
2 CONT Marriage to Jacqueline, Countess of Hainaut
2 CONT '''In 1418, he married Jacqueline, Countess of Hainaut. Jacqueline's supporters hoped that the marriage would provide a protector - in the person of John IV of Brabant - of Jacqueline's inheritance against the claims of her uncle'
2 CONC '' John III. '''Through their marriage, the counties of Holland, Zeeland and Hainaut and the duchies of Brabant and Limburg were united in a personal union.'''
2 CONT '''However, the marriage did not provide the stability sought after by Jacqueline and her supporters and eventually led to estrangement of the couple in 1420. One of the reasons was his incapability to protect her rights in Hollan
2 CONC d and Zeeland. His attempts in the first year of their marriage, such as the disastrous Siege of Dordrecht (summer of 1418), were insufficient against the force of Jacqueline's uncle, John III. Another reason why their marriage an
2 CONC d John's attempts to defend Jacqueline's rights in Holland and Zeeland did not succeed was that John's closest advisors were all part of the Cod faction, while Jacqueline's supporters were of the Hook faction in the Hook and Cod d
2 CONC isputes. This meant an inevitable political struggle. Under influence of his advisors, John appointed Jacqueline's uncle, John III, as regent (Dutch: ruwaard) of Holland in 1420. This open support of her uncle caused Jacqueline to
2 CONC  flee her husband's court and declared their marriage annulled.'''[4]
2 CONT '''After the death of John III in 1425, the county of Holland once again fell to John IV. However, he immediately appointed his cousin Philip the Good, duke of Burgundy, as the new regent as well as his heir in case he died withou
2 CONC t issue.'''[5]
2 CONT Tensions in the Duchy of Brabant
2 CONT '''That John's advisors were of the Cod faction did not only cause friction in John's marriage to Jacqueline, but also caused tensions in the Duchy of Brabant. There was a great discontent amongst the cities and the aristocracy wh
2 CONC o had been powerful under his father's reign against these powerful advisors.[6] When Jacqueline left her husband in 1420, the States of Brabant sided with her. They appointed John's brother, Philip of Saint-Pol, regent of Brabant
2 CONC .'''
2 CONT '''A forceful reaction of the duke against these measures was prevented by the uprising of the Brussels guilds. This meant that, for the time being, John had to bend to their wishes.[7] A reconciliation treaty was made in 1421 and
2 CONC  John was restored to his full ducal powers. In return, he expanded the city privileges and the powers of the States of Brabant in 1422, a measure called the Nieuw Regiment.[7] With this reconciliation, the States of Brabant suppo
2 CONC rted their duke in his fight against Jacqueline and her new husband, Humphrey, Duke of Gloucester. He never accepted the annulment of their marriage, and was post-mortem backed up by the pope in 1428.'''[8]
2 CONT '''John died in Brussels in 1427, aged 23, without issue. This meant his claims on Holland, Zeeland and Hainaut, as husband of Jacqueline, were transferred''' to Philip the Good. '''His duchies of Brabant and Limburg were inherite
2 CONC d by his younger brother''', Philip of Saint-Pol.
2 CONT Legacy
2 CONT '''He is often typified as a weak prince, who was easily influenced by more shrewd and politically able men, such as Philip the Good and John III of Holland. His age and inexperience would have played a major role in this characte
2 CONC rization.'''[9]
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_IV,_Duke_of_Brabant
2 CONT _______
2 CONT '''Johann IV, Duke of Brabant & Limburg, Markgraf von Antwerp & Namur'''1,2
2 CONT Last Edited	4 Sep 2011
2 CONT M, #48441, '''b. 11 June 1403, d. 17 April 1427'''
2 CONT '''Father'''	Anton, Duke of Brabant & Limburg, Markgraf von Antwerp, Count of Rethel1 b. Aug 1384, d. 25 Oct 1415
2 CONT '''Mother'''	Jeanne de Luxembourg1 b. c 1383, d. 12 Aug 1407
2 CONT '''     Johann IV, Duke of Brabant & Limburg, Markgraf von Antwerp & Namur was born on 11 June 1403.1 He married Jacqueline of Holland''', daughter of Wilhelm VI, Count of Holland, Hennegau, Seeland, & Straubing, Lord of Arkel and
2 CONC  Margarethe de Burgundy, '''on 10 April 1418 at Den Haag, Netherlands.1,3,2 Johann IV, Duke of Brabant & Limburg, Markgraf von Antwerp & Namur and an unknown person were divorced on 7 March 1422; A divorce granted by Anti-Pope Ben
2 CONC edict XIII.4,2 He died on 17 April 1427 at Brussels, Belgium, at age 23.'''1
2 CONT '''Family'''
2 CONT '''Jacqueline of Holland''' b. 16 Jul 1401, d. 9 Oct 1436
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT 1.[S11569] Europaische Stammtafeln, by Wilhelm Karl, Prinz zu Isenburg, Vol. II, Tafel 27.
2 CONT 2.[S16] Douglas Richardson, Magna Carta Ancestry, 2nd Edition, Vol. II, p. 550-551.
2 CONT 3.[S5] Douglas Richardson, Plantagenet Ancestry, p. 432.
2 CONT 4.[S11575] The Lineage and Ancestry of H.R.H. Prince Charles, Prince of Wales, by Gerald Paget, Vol. I, p. 27.
2 CONT From: https://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p1612.htm#i48441
2 CONT ______
2 CONT '''Jean IV de Valois, Duc de Brabant'''1 
2 CONT M, #107519, '''b. between 1400 and 1403, d. circa 1426'''
2 CONT Last Edited=2 Jul 2009
2 CONT '''     Jean IV de Valois, Duc de Brabant was born between 1400 and 1403.1 He was the son''' of Antoine de Valois, Duc de Brabant and Jeanne de Luxembourg.2 '''He married Jacqueline von Bayern-Straubing, Comtesse de Hainaut et Gra
2 CONC vin van Holland en Zeeland''', daughter of Wilhelm IV Herzog von Bayern-Straubing Comte de Hainaut et Graaf van Holland en Zeeland and Marguerite de Bourgogne, '''on 10 March 1418.1 He died circa 1426.'''1
2 CONT '''     He gained the title of Duc de Brabant in 1415.1 His marriage to Jacqueline von Bayern-Straubing, Comtesse de Hainaut et Gravin van Holland en Zeeland was annulled in 1421/22 by the Anti-Pope, Benedict XIII.'''1
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT 1.[S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Families: The Complete Genealogy (London, U.K.: The Bodley Head, 1999), page 125. Hereinafter cited as Britain's Royal Families.
2 CONT 2.[S16] Jirí Louda and Michael MacLagan, Lines of Succession: Heraldry of the Royal Families of Europe, 2nd edition (London, U.K.: Little, Brown and Company, 1999), table 75. Hereinafter cited as Lines of Succession.
2 CONT From: https://www.thepeerage.com/p10752.htm#i107519
2 CONT ____________
