1 NOTE {geni:about_me} a short summary from Wikipedia:
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Woodville, Queen consort of England 
2 CONT
2 CONT Tenure: 1 May 1464 – 9 April 1483
2 CONT
2 CONT Coronation: 26 May 1465
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John Grey m. c. 1452; dec. 1461
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward IV of England m. 1464; dec. 1483
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue: Thomas Grey, 1st Marquess of Dorset
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Grey
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth of York, Queen of England
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary of York
2 CONT
2 CONT Cecily of York, Viscountess Welles
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward V of England
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret of York
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard of Shrewsbury, 1st Duke of York
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne of York, Countess of Surrey
2 CONT
2 CONT George Plantagenet, Duke of Bedford
2 CONT
2 CONT Catherine of York, Countess of Devon
2 CONT
2 CONT Bridget of York
2 CONT
2 CONT House: House of York
2 CONT
2 CONT Father:  Richard Woodville, 1st Earl Rivers
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Jacquetta of Luxembourg
2 CONT
2 CONT Born: 3 February 1437
2 CONT
2 CONT Grafton Regis, Northamptonshire
2 CONT
2 CONT Died: 8 June 1492 (1492-06-08) (age 55)
2 CONT
2 CONT Bermondsey, London
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial: 
2 CONT
2 CONT St. George's Chapel, Windsor
2 CONT
2 CONT =============================================================================
2 CONT
2 CONT "Elizabeth Woodville (also spelled Wydeville or Widvile; c. 1437 – 8 June 1492) was Queen consort of England as the spouse of King Edward IV from 1464 until his death in 1483. Elizabeth was a key figure in the series of dynastic c
2 CONC ivil wars known as the Wars of the Roses. As Lady Grey (née Woodville), Elizabeth had two sons by her first husband, Sir John Grey of Groby who was killed at the Second Battle of St Albans. As the daughter of Sir Richard Woodville
2 CONC , she was the first commoner (i.e. non royal) to marry an English sovereign. It was because of this that Edward's former staunch ally Richard Neville, 16th Earl of Warwick, fell out with his cousin King Edward leading to a battle 
2 CONC of wills that finally resulted in Warwick changing sides and switching his allegiance to that of Lancaster.
2 CONT Her children included the Princes in the Tower and Elizabeth of York; the latter made her the maternal grandmother of Henry VIII and great grandmother of Queen Elizabeth I. Tradition holds that she served as a Maid of Honour to Ma
2 CONC rgaret of Anjou, but the evidence of this is uncertain."
2 CONT
2 CONT =================================================================================
