1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Henry V 'the Blond', Count of Luxemburg, Count of Arlon, Landgrave of Laroche===
2 CONT
2 CONT He was '''NOT''' a Herzog (Duke); his father's rank of Herzog (Duke) was passed to his older half-brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT As ''Henry V of Luxemburg'', he was the Graf von (Count of) Luxembourg, the Graf von (Count of) Arlon, and the Landgrave of Laroche.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was also titled ''Henry III'', the '''*'''Marquis/Graf von (Marquis/Count of) Namur, after he invaded and usurped the realm of Baldwin II of Courtenay who had been the Marquis of Namur. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''*'''The reason for this odd historic interchangeable use of both "Marquis" and "Graf/Count" for Henry's title, despite being two different titles, is an explainable quirk that can be chalked up to the typical frustrating games 
2 CONC that histrians and genealogists play with data. Namur was a County (with the ruler's rank of Graf/Count), until 1189, when Holy Roman Emperor Frederick I Barbarossa formally elevated the rulers of Namur from Graf/Count to Margrave
2 CONC /Marquis; from that point forward, the realm was NO LONGER the County of Namur and its ruler no longer the Graf/Count of Namur; they were officially styled the realm of the ''Margraviate of Namur'' and its ruler became the ''Margr
2 CONC ave/Marquis of Namur''. However, historians frustratingly continued to refer to the Margraviate & Margrave/Marquis of Namur by its pre-1189 styles of County & Graf/Count of Namur. Hence the interchangeable use of Marquis and Graf/
2 CONC Count of Namur for Henry. The upshot of this is that Henry, as Henry III, was OFFICIALLY the Marquis of Namur because the rank of Count of Namur was abolished and replaced in 1189.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son and successor of Waleran III of Limburg and his wife Ermesinda of Luxembourg. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1240 Henry married Margaret of Bar, daughter of Henry II of Bar by his wife Philippa of Dreux who was the great-granddaughter of King Louis VI of France. 
2 CONT Henry's marriage to Margaret brought him Ligny-en-Barrois as her dowry, though, by a clause in the marriage contract, it remained under the feudal suzerainty of the County of Bar. In contempt of this, Henry paid homage in 1256 to 
2 CONC his brother-in-law, Theobald II of Navarre, in his capacity as Count of Champagne. Theobald took advantage of the conflict that was then raging between Frederick III Duke of Lorraine and the Bishop of Metz. Henry was a partisan of
2 CONC  the Duke, and so Theobald took the side of the Bishop. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry was captured in battle at Prény on 14 September 1266. 
2 CONT Nearly two years later, om 8 September 1268, King Louis IX arbitrated between the two Counts, and Henry was freed and repossessed of Ligny, but under the suzerainty of the Barrois (as Ligny-en-Barrois).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1256, as mentioned earlier, Henry seized the Margraviate of Namur while its the reigning Margrave, Baldwin II, was also reigning as Emperor in Constantinople. Baldwin relinquished his rights to Namur to Guy of Dampierre the Cou
2 CONC nt of Flanders, who retook the Margraviate from Henry. 
2 CONT Amazingly, Henry and Guy made peace, and Guy then married Henry's own daughter, Isabelle.
2 CONT
2 CONT By his wife Margaret de Bar, Henry had the following issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry VI (died 1288), Count of Luxembourg
2 CONT Waleran I (died 1288), Count of Ligny & Roussy
2 CONT Isabelle (1247–1298), married Guy of Dampierre, Count of Flanders, Marquis of Namur
2 CONT Philippa (1252–1311), married John II, Count of Holland
2 CONT Margaret
2 CONT Felicitas
2 CONT Joanna (died 1310), Abbess of Clairefontaine (now in the borders of Belgium)
2 CONT
2 CONT He also had at least three bastard sons, including:
2 CONT Henry, bastard of Luxembourg (died 1288), married Isabelle of Houffalize, heiress of Houffalize
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents:
2 CONT Waleran III of Limburg, Duke (1165–1226)
2 CONT Ermesinde de Luxembourg, Duchess-Consort (1186–1247)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Siblings:
2 CONT Heinrich von Limburg (1195–1247)
2 CONT Gerhard Limburg (1223–1298)
2 CONT Gaulus de Limburg (1225 – unknown)
2 CONT
2 CONT DEATH: 24 Dec 1281 (aged 64–65) Walloon Brabant, Belgium
2 CONT
2 CONT BURIAL
2 CONT Abbaye de Clairefontaine
2 CONT Arlon, Arrondissement d'Arlon, Luxembourg, Belgium
2 CONT
