1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''BEATRIX of England (Bordeaux 25 Jun 1242-London 24 Mar 1275, maybe bur Reading Abbey, transferred to Greyfriars Church, Newgate, London). ''' 
2 CONT d/o  HENRY III King of England & Eléonore de Provence 
2 CONT x (contract 13 Oct 1260, église de l'Abbaye royale de Saint Denis Nov 1260, Westminster Abbey [25 Dec] 1260) JEAN II Duke of Brittany, s/o JEAN I Duke of Brittany & Infanta doña Blanca de Navarra [Champagne] (3/4 Jan 1239-Lyon 16 
2 CONC Nov 1305, bur Ploërmel, Morbihan, église Notre dame du couvent des Carmes).
2 CONT *ARTHUR de Bretagne (25 Jul 1262-Château de l'Isle 1312, bur Vannes). ARTHUR II Duke of Brittany x MARIE Vicomtesse de Limoges xx  ([May 1292])  YOLANDE de Dreux
2 CONT *JEAN de Bretagne ([1266]-in Scotland 17 Jan 1334, bur Nantes, église des Franciscains).  Guardian of Scotland, Earl of Richmond
2 CONT *MARIE de Bretagne (1268-5 May 1339, bur Abbaye de Cercamp)  Heiress of Clermont.  Dame d'Elincourt et d'Arleux 1317.x (22 Jul 1292) GUY [III] de Châtillon Comte de Saint-Pol
2 CONT *PIERRE de Bretagne (1269-Jul 1312, bur Paris, église des Cordeliers).  Vicomte de Léon 
2 CONT *BLANCHE de Bretagne (1270-Château du Bois-de-Vincennes 19 Mar 1327, bur Paris église des Jacobins). x (>Nov 1281) PHILIPPE d'Artois Seigneur de Conches
2 CONT *ELEONORE de Bretagne (in England 1275-Fontevrault 16 May 1342, bur Fontevrault). Abbess of Fontevrault 1304. 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/BRITTANY.htm#MarieBretagnedied1339
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLAND,%20Kings%201066-1603.htm#Beatrixdied1275
2 CONT ----
2 CONT JEAN de Bretagne, son of JEAN I Duke of Brittany & his wife Infanta doña Blanca de Navarra [Champagne] (3/4 Jan 1239-Lyon 16 Nov 1305, bur Ploërmel, Morbihan, église Notre dame du couvent des Carmes).  The Chronicon Universum in t
2 CONC he cartulary of Sainte-Croix de Quimperlé records the birth "die martis infra octabas Sanctorum Innocentium" in 1239 of "Johannes primogenitus Johannis comitis Britannie"[415].  The Chronicon Kemperlegiense records the birth in 12
2 CONC 40 of "Iohannes primogenitus Iohannis Comitis Britanniæ"[416].  Earl of Richmond 1268, when his father resigned the earldom.  He accompanied his father and Louis IX King of France on the Crusade in 1270.  William of Tyre (Continua
2 CONC tion) records the arrival in Palestine of "Johan filz le conte de Bretaigne" 14 Sep 1272[417].  He succeeded his father in 1286 as JEAN II Duke of Brittany: the Chronicon Britannicum records the death "Id Oct" 1286 of “Joannes com
2 CONC es Britanniæ fundator abbatiæ de Precibus” and the succession of “Joannes filius eius”[418].  He received Papal recognition 1 Sep 1288.  Philippe IV King of France confirmed his title of duke of Brittany, pair de France at Courtra
2 CONC i Sep 1297[419].  King Edward I of England confiscated his English possessions in 1296, for supporting the French, but they were restored to him 1 May 1304.  He was killed by a falling wall, while leading the Pope's horse during t
2 CONC he consecration of Pope Clement V.  The testament of "Jehan Duc de Bretaigne Comte de Richemond", dated Jul 1304, bequeathed property to “Artur mon ainzné filz...Jean de Bretaigne mon filz...Alienor de Bretaigne ma fille nonain de
2 CONC  Fontevrault...”[420].  The Chronicon Britannicum records the death “in Lugduno” 1305 of “Joannes dux Britanniæ” and the succession of “Arturus eius filius”[421]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m (contract 13 Oct 1260, église de l'Abbaye royale de Saint Denis Nov 1260, Westminster Abbey [25 Dec] 1260) BEATRIX of England, daughter of HENRY III King of England & his wife Eléonore de Provence (Bordeaux 25 Jun 1242-London
2 CONC  24 Mar 1275, maybe bur Reading Abbey, transferred to Greyfriars Church, Newgate, London).  The Annales Londonienses record the marriage in 1260 of "Johannes filius comitis Britanniæ" and "Beatricem filiam regis Angliæ"[422].  The
2 CONC  Chronicle of Thomas Wykes records the marriage “apud Westmonasterium” in 1259 of “Johannem filium et hæredum comitis Britanniæ” and “Beatriciam filiam regis”[423].  The Continuator of Florence of Worcester records the death in 12
2 CONC 75 of "Margareta regina Scotie et Beatrix comitissa Britanniæ, filiæ Henrici"[424]. 
2 CONT '''
2 CONT Duke Jean II & his wife had six children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.         ARTHUR de Bretagne (25 Jul 1262-Château de l'Isle 1312, bur Vannes).  The Chronicon Kemperlegiense records the birth "VIII Kal Aug" in 1262 of "Arturus primogenitus Iohannis Ducis Britanniæ ex Beatrice filia Regis Angli
2 CONC æ"[425].  He succeeded his father 1305 as ARTHUR II Duke of Brittany. -        see below. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.         JEAN de Bretagne ([1266]-in Scotland 17 Jan 1334, bur Nantes, église des Franciscains).  Lobineau states that Jean, second son of Duke Jean II, was born in 1266 but gives no indication of the primary source on which he 
2 CONC bases this information[426].  Appointed Lieutenant of Aquitaine 1294, and Captain of the English forces there, removed Oct 1295.  The testament of "Jehan Duc de Bretaigne Comte de Richemond", dated Jul 1304, bequeathed property to
2 CONC  “Artur mon ainzné filz...Jean de Bretaigne mon filz...Alienor de Bretaigne ma fille nonain de Fontevrault...”[427].  He was summoned to the English Parliament 24 May and 13 Jul 1305, whereby he is held to have become Lord Brittan
2 CONC y[428].  He was appointed Guardian of Scotland 15 Oct 1305.  He was created Earl of Richmond by grant of Edward I King of England 15 Oct 1306[429].  He served the kings of England until his death.  The Chronicon Britannicum record
2 CONC s the death “XVII Jan” 1333 of “nobilis princeps dominus Johannes de Britannia quondam comes Richemondiæ” and his burial “in domo Fratrum Minorum Nann.”[430].  Inquisitions following a writ dated 13 Feb 1334, following the death o
2 CONC f "John de Britannia Earl of Richemund", record “heir unknown because the said earl was born in foreign parts and was never married” when listing his numerous properties in England[431]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.         MARIE de Bretagne (1268-5 May 1339, bur Abbaye de Cercamp).  The marriage contract between “Jehan Duc de Bretagne Conte de Richemont...Damoiselle Marie fille dud. Duc de Bretagne” and “Hues de Chastillon Conte de Blois 
2 CONC Sires de Avenes…Guy nostre…frere” is dated 22 Jul 1292[432].  Heiress of Clermont.  Dame d'Elincourt et d'Arleux 1317.  m (22 Jul 1292) GUY [III] de Châtillon Comte de Saint-Pol, son of GUY [II] de Châtillon Comte de Saint-Pol & h
2 CONC is wife Beatrix de Brabant (-6 Apr 1317). 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.         PIERRE de Bretagne (1269-Jul 1312, bur Paris, église des Cordeliers).  Vicomte de Léon until 1293.  A charter dated Oct 1293 records that “Petrum de Britannia filium suum” transferred “Leonie...vicecomitatum” to “Johann
2 CONC es comes Britannie”[433].  Kerrebrouck records that Pierre died in 1312 from a wound received in his leg from a horse´s kick and was buried “en l´église des Cordeliers de Paris” but does not cite the corresponding primary sources[
2 CONC 434]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.         BLANCHE de Bretagne (1270-Château du Bois-de-Vincennes 19 Mar 1327, bur Paris église des Jacobins).  The Gesta Philippi Tertia Francorum Regis of Guillaume de Nangis records that "comes Attrebati Robertus...[filium] Phi
2 CONC lippum" married "filiam Johannis comitis Britanniæ Blancham, neptem Edoardi regis Angliæ"[435].  Dame de Brie-Comte-Robert.  Philippe IV King of France granted revenue to "consanguinea nostra Blancha...J. ducis Britannie filia" fo
2 CONC r “Margarete filie sue...Ludovici comitis Ebroicensis germani nostri...uxori...maritagii” by charter dated Jul 1303[436].  Dame de Neuvy et de Pandy 1304.  m (after Nov 1281) PHILIPPE d'Artois Seigneur de Conches, son of ROBERT II
2 CONC  "le Bon/le Noble" Comte d'Artois [Capet] & his first wife Amicie de Courtenay (1269-near Furnes 11 Sep 1298, bur Paris, église des Jacobins).  
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.         ELEONORE de Bretagne (in England 1275-Fontevrault 16 May 1342, bur Fontevrault).  A manuscript Memorandum records the donation of “Manerio de Chadelworth” in Berkshire by “domina Alianora regina Angliæ, mater regis”, an
2 CONC d names “Alianora de Britan. filia domini Johannis ducis Britan.” as a nun at Amesbury[437].  Nun at Fontevrault [1291].  The testament of "Jehan Duc de Bretaigne Comte de Richemond", dated Jul 1304, bequeathed property to “Artur 
2 CONC mon ainzné filz...Jean de Bretaigne mon filz...Alienor de Bretaigne ma fille nonain de Fontevrault...”[438].  Abbess of Fontevrault 1304. 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/BRITTANY.htm#MarieBretagnedied1339
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLAND,%20Kings%201066-1603.htm#Beatrixdied1275
2 CONT ----
2 CONT "Beatrice of England (25 June 1242[1] – 24 March 1275), also known as Beatrice de Dreux, was a Princess of England as the daughter of King Henry III of England and Eleanor of Provence. Her siblings were Edward I of England, Margar
2 CONC et, Queen of Scotland, Edmund Crouchback, 1st Earl of Lancaster, Richard of England, John of England, Katherine of England, William of England, and Henry of England. She and her family were members of the Royal house of Plantagene
2 CONC t, which first ruled in the 12th century and was founded by Henry II of England."
2 CONT
