1 NAME Eberhard V /von der Marck/
2 GIVN Eberhard V
2 SURN von der Marck
2 _MARNM von der Marck
1 NAME Eberhard V, Graf von der Marck und Arenberg
1 SEX M
1 DEAT
2 DATE 22 NOV 1531
1 TITL Graf
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_La_Marck
2 CONT
2 CONT Adolph de la Marck (1288–1344), Prince-Bishop of Liège
2 CONT Adolph II of the Marck (died 1347), Count of the Marck, son of Engelbert I of the Marck
2 CONT Engelbert III of the Mark (1333–1391), Count of the Marck, son of Count Adolph II
2 CONT Adolf III of the Marck (c. 1334–1394), Prince-Bishop of Münster, later was Archbishop-Elector of Cologne, later was Count of Cleves, later added Count of the Marck
2 CONT Engelbert de la Marck (1304–1368), Prince-Bishop of Liège, later was Archbishop-Elector of Cologne
2 CONT William I de La Marck (1445–1486), assassinated a Prince-Bishop of Liège, causing a civil war in the prince-bishopric
2 CONT Érard de La Marck (1472–1538), nephew of William I, was prince-bishop from 1506 till 1538.
2 CONT Robert II de la Marck, nephew of William I, was Duke of Bouillon, seigneur of Sedan and Fleuranges.
2 CONT Robert III de la Marck (1491–1537), son of Robert II, was Marshal of France in 1526 and historian.
2 CONT Robert IV de la Marck (1520–1556) was Duke of Bouillon and Prince of Sedan, and Marshal of France in 1547.
2 CONT William II de la Marck (1542–1578) was admiral of the Gueux de mer, the so-called 'sea beggars' who fought in the Eighty Years' War (1568–1648). He was the great-grandson of William I de la Marck.
2 CONT Anne of Cleves, 4th wife of King Henry VIII of England, was a member of this house, daughter of John III.
2 CONT In 1591 the heiress of one of the collateral lines of the family, Charlotte de la Marck, was married to Henri de La Tour d'Auvergne, Marshal of France. In 1594 Charlotte died without issue, and her claims to Bouillon passed to her
2 CONC  husband.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis Pierre Engelbert, Comte de la Marck (1674–1750)[1]
2 CONT Louis Engelbert, Comte de la Marck (1701–1773)[1] He was the last male descendant of the Counts de la Marck, so his title Comte de la Marck was passed on to his grandson through his daughter, Louise-Marguerite, who married Charles
2 CONC  Marie Raymond of Arenberg.[2]
2 CONT Auguste Marie Raymond, Comte de la Marck (1753–1833)
2 CONT
2 CONT Adolf I (d. 1249), first documented as comes de Marca in 1202
2 CONT Engelbert I (d. 1277)
2 CONT Eberhard (d. 1308)
2 CONT Engelbert II (d. 1328)
2 CONT Adolf II (d. 1346), married Margaret of Cleves in 1332
2 CONT Engelbert III (d. 1391)
2 CONT Adolf III (1334–1394), Count of Cleves from 1368 → see below
2 CONT Engelbert (1304–1368), Prince-Bishop of Liège 1345–1364, Archbishop of Cologne 1364–68
2 CONT Eberhard I (d. about 1378), Count of Arenberg → see below
2 CONT Adolf (1288–1344), Prince-Bishop of Liège 1313–1344
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eberhard_I,_Count_of_the_Mark
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Eberhard I (c. 1255–4 July 1308) was a German nobleman. He was Count of the Mark from 1277 until his death. He was the son of Engelbert I, Count of the Mark and Kunigunde of Blieskastel (died 1265), daughter of Count Henry I of Bl
2 CONC ieskastel.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1277, Count Herman of Lohn abducted Eberhard's father Engelbert I, Count of the Mark near Tecklenburg and imprisoned him in the Castle of Bredevoort, where he later died. In 1278 Eberhard took revenge and conquered the castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1281, Eberhard formed an alliance with the Counts of Berg, Cleve and Jülich against the Electorate of Cologne and gained the independence of the County of Mark from the Archbishop of Cologne after the victory in the Battle of
2 CONC  Worringen in 1288. He also obtained Brakel, Westhofen and Waltrop. Eberhard died 4 July 1308 and was buried in Fröndenberg Monastery.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and children
2 CONT Eberhard married first about 29 January 1273 to Irmgard of Berg (c.1256–24 March 1294), daughter of Adolf VII of Berg. They had seven children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Engelbert II (c.1275–1328)
2 CONT Adolph II of La Marck, Prince-bishop of Liège (1278–1344)
2 CONT Margaret (c.1280–after 14 August 1327), married in 1299 Gerhard of Katzenelnbogen (c.1270–1311/12)
2 CONT Kunigunde († after 25 February 1343), married in 1320 Dietrich II of Heinsberg-Blankenburg († 1361)
2 CONT Irmgard
2 CONT
2 CONT Konrad (1291/94–1353), married Elisabeth of Cleves
2 CONT
2 CONT Catharine, abbess of Froendenberg (born c.1293)
2 CONT
2 CONT Eberhard married second Maria of Looz and had:
2 CONT
2 CONT Richarda, married John III of Reifferscheidt-Bedburg
2 CONT
2 CONT Johanna, married Philip IV of Reifferscheidt-Wildenberg
2 CONT
2 CONT Engelbert I, Lord of Loverval
2 CONT
2 CONT Eberhard I, Count of the Mark
2 CONT House of La Marck
2 CONT Born: c. 1255 Died: 1308
2 CONT
2 CONT Preceded by
2 CONT Engelbert I
2 CONT Count of the Mark
2 CONT 1277–1308	
2 CONT
2 CONT Succeeded by
2 CONT Engelbert II
