1 NOTE {geni:about_me} heiress of Logiealmond (dau of John Logie of Logiealmond)
2 CONT 2. Thomas Hay of Logie, killed at the Battle of Flodden (d. 9 Sep 1513), mar. 1493 Margaret Logie (mar. (2) Robert Murray), dau. of Lyon Logie of that Ilk, and had issue:
2 CONT # 1a. George Hay, later 7th Earl of Erroll
2 CONT # 1a. Beatrix Hay, mar. bef. 30 Mar 1522 Walter Bonar of Keltie
2 CONT
2 CONT Links
2 CONT * http://www.cracroftspeerage.co.uk/online/content/erroll1452.htm#ERROLL_1452_3
2 CONT * http://www.genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00112073&tree=LEO
2 CONT * http://www.thepeerage.com/p2630.htm
2 CONT ------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT "Thomas Hay of Logie, who was slain at Flodden with his brother on September 9, 1513, married in 1493 Margaret Logie, heiress of Logiealmond in Perthshire, of which lands he had a charter upon his resignation, and precept from Kin
2 CONC g James lV for infefting him and her in the barony on October 4, 1493. She survived him and married Robert Murray. By her first marriage Margaret had a son George, who became 7th Earl of Errol, and a daughter, Beatrix, who married
2 CONC  Walter Bonar of Keltie.
2 CONT NOTE:- Margaret Logie was the daughter of John Logie of Logiealmond and Euphemia Boyd. Euphemia was the daughter of Alexander Boyd, Earl of Kilmarnock at that time. It would appear that this was a marriage of cousins as in 1368 a 
2 CONC Thomas Hay of Tullibody married Margaret Logie—see entry under that date. The Hay family thus gained these lands previously owned by that branch of the Logie family.
2 CONT It should be noted that at this time in Scotland heritable property passed to the eldest child irrespective of sex and that therefore when the eldest child was a female the lands would pass to her and then to her husband and child
2 CONC ren."
2 CONT [https://www.leedrew.com/2013/06/the-logie-surname-and-early-family....]
1 FAMS @F6000000001013150089@
