1 NOTE {geni:about_me} George Hay, 7th Earl of Erroll1 M, #26290, d. 30 January 1573/74
2 CONT
2 CONT George Hay, 7th Earl of Erroll|d. 30 Jan 1573/74|p2629.htm#i26290|Thomas Hay|d. 9 Sep 1513|p2630.htm#i26293|Margaret Logie||p2630.htm#i26294|William Hay, 3rd Earl of Erroll|d. 14 Jan 1506/7|p2048.htm#i20476|Lady Isabella Gordon|d.
2 CONC  b 1485|p10827.htm#i108268|Lyon Logie of that Ilk||p2630.htm#i26295||||
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Edited=8 Apr 2009
2 CONT      George Hay, 7th Earl of Erroll was the son of Thomas Hay and Margaret Logie.2 He married, firstly, Margaret Robertson, daughter of Alexander Robertson of Struan and Isobel Stewart, circa 12 November 1528.2 He married, secondl
2 CONC y, Helen Bruce, daughter of Walter Bruce, circa 12 June 1561.3,2 He died on 30 January 1573/74.2
2 CONT      George Hay, 7th Earl of Erroll succeeded to the title of 8th Lord Hay [S., 1430] on 11 April 1541.2 He succeeded to the title of 7th Earl of Erroll [S., 1452] on 11 April 1541.1,2 He held the office of Lord-Lieutenant of Cent
2 CONC ral Scotland in 1559.2 In 1567 he refused to officiate as Lord High Constable at king James IV's coronation, in support for Mary, Queen of Scots.2 He was invested as a Privy Counsellor (P.C.) [Scotland].2 Child of George Hay, 7th 
2 CONC Earl of Erroll and Helen Bruce 1.Lady Elizabeth Hay Children of George Hay, 7th Earl of Erroll and Margaret Robertson 1.Lady Elizabeth Hay+4 2.Andrew Hay, 8th Earl of Erroll+2 d. 8 Oct 1585 3.Lady Margaret Hay+1 4.John Hay2 d. May
2 CONC  1579 5.Laurence Hay2 6.George Hay+2 7.Lady Beatrix Hay2 Citations 1.[S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scot
2 CONC land, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extinct or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume I, page 160. Hereinafter cited as
2 CONC  The Complete Peerage. 2.[S37] Charles Mosley, editor, Burke's Peerage, Baronetage & Knightage, 107th edition, 3 volumes (Wilmington, Delaware, U.S.A.: Burke's Peerage (Genealogical Books) Ltd, 2003), volume 1, page 1336. Hereinaf
2 CONC ter cited as Burke's Peerage and Baronetage, 107th edition. 3.[S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume I, page 383. 4.[S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume VIII, page 479.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT George Hay, 7th Earl of Erroll was the son of Thomas Hay and Margaret Logie. He married, firstly, Margaret Robertson, daughter of Alexander Robertson, 9th of Glenerochie and Lady Elizabeth Stewart, circa 12 November 1528.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, secondly, Helen Bruce, daughter of Walter Bruce, circa 12 June 1561.
2 CONT
2 CONT George was served heir to his mother and seised in the barony of Logiealmond in Oct. 1536.[3] He succeeded to the title of 7th Lord Hay and to the title of 7th Earl of Erroll on 11 April 1541.[4],[1] He was served heir to his cous
2 CONC in William, Earl of Erroll in the lordship and barony of Erroll which had been in the King's hands for over 19 years.[5] He had charters to the lordship of Erroll and to the lands and baronies of Capeth, Inchiref and Fossoquhy, Pe
2 CONC rthshire, Cowrie in Kincardine, Cassingray in Fife, and Dronlaw in Forfar, all of which was made into the barony of Erroll 5 Dec. 1541. On 13 Dec. 1541 he received charters to the barony of Slains, Aberdeenshire, Innerpeffer, Forf
2 CONC arshire and the office of constabulary.[6] He granted a bond to pay 4,000 marks to the King to secure Helen, Countess of Erroll, 400 marks yearly, and to marry his oldest son to Jean Hay the last earl's only daughter dated 2 Sept.
2 CONC  1541.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the office of Lord-Lieutenant of Central Scotland between the water of Erne and the north water on 22 July 1559 and in 1562 he was appointed along with several others to resist the Earl of Huntly. [8] In 1567 he refused to
2 CONC  officiate as Lord High Constable at king James IV's coronation, in support for Mary, Queen of Scots.[1] He was invested as a Privy Counsellor for Scotland.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on 30 January 1573/74.
