1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Sir John Drummond, 11th of Lennox1,2
2 CONT * M, #102467, b. 1318, d. 1373
2 CONT * Last Edited=2 Mar 2005
2 CONT *http://www.thepeerage.com/p10247.htm#i102467
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John Drummond, 11th of Lennox was born in 1318.2 He was the son of Sir Malcolm Drummond, 10th Thane of Lennox and Margaret de Graham.2 He married Mary Montifex, daughter of Sir William de Montifex.2 He died in 1373.2  Sir John
2 CONC  Drummond, 11th of Lennox lived at Strobhall, Scotland.1
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Sir John Drummond, 11th of Lennox and Mary Montifex
2 CONT
2 CONT *     * Dougal Drummond 2
2 CONT *     * Annabel Drummond+ b. c 1350, d. c Oct 1401
2 CONT *     * Sir Malcolm Drummond b. 1351, d. 1403
2 CONT *     * Margaret Drummond b. 1354
2 CONT *     * Sir John Drummond, 12th of Lennox+ b. 1356, d. 1428
2 CONT *     * Mary Drummond b. 1357
2 CONT *     * William Drummond b. 1358
2 CONT *     * Jean Drummond b. 1362
2 CONT
2 CONT ==notes==
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://www.electricscotland.com/webclans/dtog/drummon2.html
2 CONT
2 CONT The wife of '''John, the eldest son''', was Mary, eldest daughter and co-heiress of Sir William de Montefex, with whom he got the lands of Auchterarder, Kincardine in Monteith, Cargill, and Stobhall in Perthshire. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He had four sons, 
2 CONT * Sir Malcolm, 
2 CONT * Sir John, 
2 CONT * William, and 
2 CONT * Dougal; 
2 CONT
2 CONT and three daughters - 
2 CONT * Annabella, married, in 1357, John, Earl of Carrick, high steward of Scotland, afterwards King Robert the Third, and thus became Queen of Scotland, and the mother of David, Duke of Rothesay, starved to death in the palace of Falk
2 CONC land, in 1402, and of James the First, as well as of three daughters; 
2 CONT * Margaret, married to Sir Colin Campbell of Lochow, 
2 CONT * Jean, to Stewart of Donally, and 
2 CONT * Mary, to Macdonald of the Isles.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT The Drummonds, according to unvarying tradition, are of Hungarian origin, Maurice, the first of that family who settled in Scotland, having come from that country in 1066 with Edgar the Atheling and Margaret, his sister, afterward
2 CONC s wife of King Malcolm III of Scotland.  Maurice adopted the name Drummond from the Gaelic 'druim' and 'monadh', that is 'back of the mountain'.  Maurice was the son of George, a younger son of Andreas, King of Hungary. Andreas co
2 CONC uld trace his ancestry to Arpad, the Magyar king who conquered Hungary (d. 907), who m. a dt. of Jaroslav, the king of Novgorod and Kiev. Jaroslav was descended from the Swedish Viking Rurik (d. 870), who became king of Novgorod. 
2 CONC The Swedish conquerors were called the Rus, or fair-haired, from which the word 'Russia' is derived
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT *[http://books.google.com/books?id=3iswAAAAYAAJ&lpg=PA261&ots=98AqJYEEQY&dq=thomas%20of%20balfrone%20Drummond&pg=PA260#v=onepage&q=thomas%20of%20balfrone%20Drummond&f=false The Genealogy of the Most Noble and Ancient House of Drum
2 CONC mond] (Google eBook) William Drummond Strathallan (1st viscount), William Drummond Priv. print., 1889.  Page 261
2 CONT *http://www.stirnet.com/genie/data/british/dd/drummond01.php#link1
2 CONT *https://www.clanmacfarlanegenealogy.info/genealogy/TNGWebsite/getperson.php?personID=I685&tree=CC
2 CONT * http://www.thepeerage.com/p10247.htm#i102467 cites 
2 CONT *  G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extinct or
2 CONC  Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume I, page 155. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT *Derek Hughes, "re: 1st Lord Drummond," e-mail message from <e-mail address> (unknown address) to Darryl Lundy, 22 December 2004, 13 February 2005 and 2 March 2005. Hereinafter cited as "re: 1st Lord Drummond".
2 CONT *Charles Mosley, editor, Burke's Peerage and Baronetage, 106th edition, 2 volumes (Crans, Switzerland: Burke's Peerage (Genealogical Books) Ltd, 1999), volume 1, page 104. Hereinafter cited as Burke's Peerage and Baronetage, 106th
2 CONC  edition.
