1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Mary Montifex==
2 CONT * F, #102464, b. 1325
2 CONT * Last Edited=2 Mar 2005
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary Montifex was born in 1325.2 She was the daughter of Sir William de Montifex.1 She married Sir John Drummond, 11th of Lennox, son of Sir Malcolm Drummond, 10th Thane of Lennox and Margaret de Graham.2
2 CONT
2 CONT Her married name became Drummond.2 She was also known as Mary Montfichet.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Mary Montifex and Sir John Drummond, 11th of Lennox
2 CONT *     * Dougal Drummond 2
2 CONT *     * Annabel Drummond+ b. c 1350, d. c Oct 14011
2 CONT *     * Sir Malcolm Drummond b. 1351, d. 14032
2 CONT *     * Margaret Drummond b. 13542
2 CONT *     * Sir John Drummond, 12th of Lennox+ b. 1356, d. 14282
2 CONT *     * Mary Drummond b. 13572
2 CONT *     * William Drummond b. 13582
2 CONT *     * Jean Drummond b. 13622
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT From Genealogical and Heraldic Dictionary of the Peerage and Baronetage of the British Empire (Google eBook) Bernard Burke Harrison, 1865 - Nobility - 1323 pages. Page 877:
2 CONT
2 CONT >"Sir John Drummond, m, in 1360, Mary, dau. of Sir William de Montifex, heiress of Stobhall, and of large possessions in Perthshire. His eldest dau. Annabella, a lady of great beauty and merit, m. King Robert III., was crowned wit
2 CONC h him at Scone, in September, 1390, and is ancestress of Queen Victoria, and of most of the crowned heads of Europe."
2 CONT
2 CONT Known to be alive at January 31st, 1375 and died shortly afterwards
2 CONT
2 CONT From [https://sites.google.com/site/brettdrummond2/drummondclan Drummond Clan]
2 CONT
2 CONT >"Meantime, by the marriage of Sir John Drummond, grandson of the Drummond who fought at Bannockburn, to Mary the daughter and heiress of Sir William de Montifex, the family had come into possession of Stobhall on the Tay and larg
2 CONC e possessions in Perthshire, and a further alliance with the royal house was made when Sir John’s eldest daughter Annabella became the wife of King Robert III., and was crowned with him at Scone in September, 1390. Through this ma
2 CONC rriage all the succeeding Kings of Scotland and of Britain have been descended from the House of Drummond, and there is Drummond blood in the veins of most of the crowned heads of Europe."
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Citations
2 CONT * 1. Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Family: A Complete Genealogy (London, U.K.: The Bodley Head, 1999), page 227. Hereinafter cited as Britain's Royal Family.
2 CONT * 2. Derek Hughes, "re: 1st Lord Drummond," e-mail message from <e-mail address> (unknown address) to Darryl Lundy, 22 December 2004, 13 February 2005 and 2 March 2005. Hereinafter cited as "re: 1st Lord Drummond".
2 CONT *Genealogical memoir of the most noble and ancient house of Drummond, David Malcolm, (Drummond-Genealogical memoir of the most noble and ancient house of Drummond 1808 by David Malcolm.pdf on file E://genealogy/books). [https://bo
2 CONC oks.google.com/books?id=hTEwAAAAYAAJ&vq=Montifex&pg=PA30#v=onepage&q&f=true GoogleBooks]
2 CONT
