1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *'''William Crichton, 3rd Lord Crichton1,2,3,4
2 CONT *'''M, #65463, d. before 23 October 1493
2 CONT *Father     James Crichton, 2nd Lord Crichton, Earl of Moray, Chamberlain of Scotland1,2,3,4 d. Aug 1454
2 CONT *Mother     Janet Dunbar1,2,3,4 d. b 19 Jan 1506
2 CONT *'''     William Crichton, 3rd Lord Crichton married Margaret Stewart, daughter of James II Stewart, King of Scotland and Marie of Guelders, DID NOT MARRY; They had 1 illegitimate daughter (Margaret, wife of William Todrig, of Geo
2 CONC rge Halkerston, & of George Leslie, 4th Earl of Rothes).5,6,3,4 William Crichton, 3rd Lord Crichton was born at of Crichton, Edinburgh, Scotland.1,2 He married Marion Livingston, daughter of James Livingston, 1st Lord Livingston a
2 CONC nd Marian of Berwick, before 1478.7,1 William Crichton, 3rd Lord Crichton died before 23 October 1493.1,2,4
2 CONT *'''Family 1      Marion Livingston d. b 23 Oct 1493
2 CONT *Child
2 CONT **Sir James Crichton+8 d. c 1536
2 CONT *'''Family 2      Margaret Stewart d. a 1503
2 CONT *Child
2 CONT **Margaret Crichton+9,3 b. c 1485, d. bt 31 May 1542 - 1546
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT * 1.[S11568] The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain, and the United Kingdom, by George Edward Cokayne, Vol. III, p. 539.
2 CONT * 2.[S11565] The Scots Peerage, Vol. III, edited by Sir James Balfour Paul, p. 64-67.
2 CONT * 3.[S16] Douglas Richardson, Magna Carta Ancestry, 2nd Edition, Vol. III, p. 586.
2 CONT * 4.[S4] Douglas Richardson, Royal Ancestry, Vol. I, p. 664.
2 CONT * 5.[S11565] The Scots Peerage, Vol. III, edited by Sir James Balfour Paul, p. 64-66.
2 CONT * 6.[S11563] The Scots Peerage, Vol. I, edited by Sir James Balfour Paul, p. 20.
2 CONT * 7.[S11565] The Scots Peerage, Vol. III, edited by Sir James Balfour Paul, p. 64.
2 CONT * 8.[S11566] The Scots Peerage, Vol. IV, edited by Sir James Balfour Paul, p. 123-124.
2 CONT * 9.[S11565] The Scots Peerage, Vol. III, edited by Sir James Balfour Paul, p. 66-68.
2 CONT *From: http://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p2179.htm#i65463 _____________________
2 CONT *'''William Crichton, 3rd Lord Crichton1
2 CONT *M, #466434
2 CONT *Last Edited=22 Nov 2012 
2 CONT *'''     William Crichton, 3rd Lord Crichton is the son of James Crichton, 2nd Lord Crichton and Lady Janet Dunbar.1 He married Margaret Stewart, daughter of James II Stewart, King of Scotland and Marie van Egmont-Gelre.1
2 CONT *'''     He gained the title of 3rd Lord Crichton.
2 CONT *'''Children of William Crichton, 3rd Lord Crichton and Margaret Stewart
2 CONT ** 1.Sir James Crichton of Frendraught+2
2 CONT ** 2.Margaret Crichton+3 b. b 1507, d. b 1546
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT * 1.[S22] Sir Bernard Burke, C.B. LL.D., A Genealogical History of the Dormant, Abeyant, Forfeited, and Extinct Peerages of the British Empire, new edition (1883; reprint, Baltimore, Maryland: Genealogical Publishing Company, 1978
2 CONC ), page 145. Hereinafter cited as Burkes Extinct Peerage.
2 CONT * 2.[S5792] Robin Dening, "re: Rochfort Family," e-mail message to Darryl Roger LUNDY (101053), 2 January 2012. Hereinafter cited as "re: Rochfort Family."
2 CONT * 3.[S37] Volume 1, page 601. See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S37]
2 CONT *From: http://www.thepeerage.com/p46644.htm#i466434 ________________________________
2 CONT *'''Margaret Stewart (born c. 1455, date of death unknown) was the younger daughter of James II of Scotland and Mary of Guelders. Once engaged to the Lancastrian Prince of Wales, Margaret instead became the mistress of William Cri
2 CONC chton, 3rd Lord Crichton (an enemy of her brother, James III), and the mother of his illegitimate daughter, Margaret Crichton, later Countess of Rothes, and possibly his son, Sir James Crichton, progenitor of the Viscounts of Fren
2 CONC draught.[1] Margaret and Lord Crichton may have been married later, after the death of Crichton's wife.
2 CONT *Margaret was born between 1453 and 1460[2][3] in Scotland, the daughter of James II of Scotland and Mary of Guelders. She had five siblings, including James III, who ascended the Scottish throne in 1460 upon their father's accide
2 CONC ntal death by an exploding cannon. Margaret's mother died in 1463, leaving her an orphan at probably less than ten years old.
2 CONT *During the Wars of the Roses, Margaret was briefly engaged to Edward of Westminster, Prince of Wales, the only son of Henry VI of England and Margaret of Anjou. However, the engagement was called off by her mother due to politica
2 CONC l pressure from Edward IV of England and Philip III, Duke of Burgundy. In 1476, she was proposed by James III to George Plantagenet, 1st Duke of Clarence, and she was afterward to have been married to Anthony Woodville, 2nd Earl R
2 CONC ivers, brother-in-law of Edward IV; but neither of these alliances took place.[4]
2 CONT *'''William Crichton, 3rd Lord Crichton of Auchingoul (grandson of Lord Chancellor Crichton) is said to have "deliberately debauched Margaret" (James III's youngest and favourite sister), after discovering that his wife had been s
2 CONC educed by the king.[5] Regardless of the truth of this story, Margaret did become Lord Crichton's mistress, which led to her disgrace and reputation for immorality and corruption. Their illegitimate daughter, also named Margaret, 
2 CONC was born between 1478 and 1485 and raised in the royal court.[6] Margaret may have had a son also, James Crichton, who married Catherine Borthwick, the eldest daughter of William, Lord Borthwick; however, James might have been the
2 CONC  son of Lord Crichton's wife.
2 CONT *'''Lord Crichton joined Margaret's brother Alexander Stewart, Duke of Albany, in his rebellion against Margaret's eldest brother, the unpopular King James III. On behalf of the duke, Lord Crichton garrisoned his "very ancient and
2 CONC  magnificent" castle of Crichton, for which his lands and titles were forfeited by the Parliament of Scotland in 1484, when Albany was sentenced for treason.[1] His castle was granted to a minion of James III, Sir John Ramsay of B
2 CONC almain.
2 CONT *According to historian George Buchanan (who was "always hostile"[7] to the Stewarts), Margaret had an incestuous relationship with her brother the king. Contemporary writers were unsympathetic in their descriptions of the princes
2 CONC s. One writer calls her "a person, although young and beautiful, of depraved character, being even charged with too much familiarity with her own brother."[4] Another writer agrees, conceding that Margaret was "a Princess of great
2 CONC  beauty, but of a reputation that was more than loose."[5]
2 CONT *Sir Walter Scott writes: 
2 CONT **'''[After garrisoning his castle and fleeing to England,] William, the hereditary Lord of Crichton, pined in exile, from which he is said to have been recalled under the following circumstances: The Lady of Crichton, says Buchan
2 CONC an, died soon after her husband's flight to England; and the king, desirous to hide the disgrace of his favourite sister, who was almost frantic for the loss of her paramour, recalled Crichton from banishment, on condition of his 
2 CONC marrying the Princess Margaret. They were wedded accordingly, and Crichton seems to have obtained restitution of that part of his fortune which descended to him by his mother, the Barony of Frendraught, namely, in the North, which
2 CONC  from this time became his residence, and that of his successors. Crichton was reconciled to the king, and admitted to his presence at Inverness, during an expedition which James made towards the North near the conclusion of his r
2 CONC eign. He therefore entertained hopes of obtaining a full pardon, but neither of the brothers-in-law long survived the interview. Crichton died at Inverness, where, according to Buchanan, his monument was extant in the time of the 
2 CONC historian. It seems uncertain whether the son who survived William Lord Crichton was the child of the Princess Margaret; but he left a daughter, their undoubted issue, who became Countess of Rothes.
2 CONT *'''If Scott's account is accurate, Margaret, then Lady Crichton, may have spent the rest of her life at Crichton's residence "in the North", at the Barony of Frendraught.
2 CONT *Margaret's daughter, Margaret Crichton, possibly had four daughters by her third husband, George Leslie, 4th Earl of Rothes, Ambassador to Denmark (whom she married twice), including Agnes Leslie, Countess of Morton, and two sons
2 CONC , William Rothes and Norman Leslie, Master of Rothes, whose rights of inheritance were forfeited as a result of both having been implicated in the murder of Cardinal Beaton in 1546. Their father, the 4th Earl of Rothes, was tried 
2 CONC and acquitted of the same crime. However, the mother of these children might have been a different wife of Rothes.
2 CONT *Margaret's possible granddaughter Lady Margaret Leslie married Archibald Douglas, 8th Earl of Angus in 1575 and divorced him in 1587, probably because of her "infertility". (He remarried two weeks later.)[8] Another granddaughter
2 CONC , Lady Helen Leslie, had several children with Mark Kerr, the abbot of Newbattle, including Mark Kerr, 1st Earl of Lothian.[9]
2 CONT *Among her numerous possible descendants are included Diana, Princess of Wales, and Camilla, Duchess of Cornwall.
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Margaret_Stewart_(born_c._1455)
2 CONT *_____________________________________
2 CONT *Links
2 CONT * http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crichton_Castle
2 CONT * http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lord_Crichton
2 CONT * http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clan_Crichton
2 CONT * http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Panter
2 CONT * http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patrick_Paniter
2 CONT * http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agnes_Leslie,_Countess_of_Morton
2 CONT * http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Leslie,_4th_Earl_of_Rothes
2 CONT * http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norman_Leslie_(soldier)
2 CONT * http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_revocations_of_appointments_to_orders_and_awarded_decorations_and_medals_of_the_United_Kingdom
2 CONT
