1 TITL (Gen 15)
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Protagonist of the Secret Vow & Medicean Envoy:''' ''Famously known in her youth as “La Petite Pienne,” Jeanne d’Halluin was the central figure in a secret matrimonial vow whose private resolution escalated into
2 CONC  a national crisis of state. While her Flemish cousins managed frontier marchlands, Jeanne’s clandestine betrothal to the heir of the House of Montmorency reached the Papacy and fundamentally reshaped French marriage laws. Her eve
2 CONC ntual alliance with Florimond II/III Robertet anchored the Piennes branch within the highest echelons of the Valois administrative machine, a position she later consolidated through her second marriage to royal diplomat Philippe d
2 CONC 'Angennes, Seigneur du Fargis.''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''(Gen 15) Jeanne d’Halluin, Baronne d'Alluye, Dame du Fargis; Demoiselle de Piennes et de Maignelay'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''I. Vital Overview'''
2 CONT Operational Name: Jeanne d'Halluin (also Jeanne de Halluyn, Demoiselle de Piennes, "La Petite Pienne", Baronne d'Alluye, Dame du Fargis, Madame de Robertet, Madame d'Angennes).
2 CONT Active Era (fl.): Mid-to-Late 16th Century (c. 1530–1589). High court figure and fille d’honneur / dame d’honneur to Queen Catherine de' Medici.
2 CONT Status: Generation 15 Senior Main-Stem Daughter & Royal Court Figure; Baronne d'Alluye, Dame du Fargis.
2 CONT Primary Landholdings & Caputs: Barony of Alluye (Eure-et-Loir), Le Fargis (Yvelines), and jointure claims in Piennes and Maignelay.
2 CONT Strategic Role: The Piennes representative at court; maintained deep influence within the Valois inner circle during the French Wars of Religion and transmitted high-level diplomatic status to her offspring.
2 CONT Primary Surnames & Archival Aliases: d'Halluin, de Halluyn, van Halewijn, Demoiselle de Piennes, Baronne d'Alluye, Dame du Fargis.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''II. Lineage Data'''
2 CONT House Origin: Senior Line, House of Halluin (van Halewijn / Piennes branch).
2 CONT Parents: Daughter of (Gen 14) Antoine Ier 'Antoine the Elder' d'Halluin, Seigneur de Piennes (d. 1553), and Louise de Crèvecœur, Dame de Maurepas.
2 CONT '''Marital Alliances:'''
2 CONT '''Clandestine Betrothal (Dissolved 1556/57):''' François de Montmorency (later 2nd Duc de Montmorency, Maréchal de France).
2 CONT '''Married 1st (Contract 1557):''' Florimond II/III Robertet, Baron d'Alluye, Secrétaire d'État (d. 1569).
2 CONT '''Married 2nd (c. 1570):''' Philippe d'Angennes, Seigneur du Fargis, Capitaine des Gardes de la Porte (d. 1590).
2 CONT Generational Sibling Block (Piennes / Halluin - Generation 15):
2 CONT (Gen 15) Charles I d'Halluin, 1st Duc d'Halluin, Pair de France, Governor of Metz (d. 1598).
2 CONT (Gen 15) Jean d’Halluin, Seigneur de Maintenay, de Wailly, et de Saint-Saulve (m. Marguerite de Montmorency-Fosseux).
2 CONT (Gen 15) Louise (d'Halluin) de Brouilly, Mademoiselle d'Halluin, Dame de Piennes (in part) et Mesvillers (m. François de Brouilly).
2 CONT (Gen 15) Jeanne d’Halluin, Baronne d'Alluye, Dame du Fargis (Subject).
2 CONT Primary Offspring Footprint (Generation 16):
2 CONT From Marriage 1 (Robertet): Heirs who carried forward the baronial rights of Alluye and the seigneuries of Bury and Brou.
2 CONT From Marriage 2 (d'Angennes):
2 CONT Charles d'Angennes, Comte du Fargis (d. 1640), French Ambassador to Spain under Louis XIII.
2 CONT Marie d'Angennes, (m. Antoine de Lenoncourt, Marquis de Blainville).
2 CONT Jurisdictional Footprint: Royal Court of France, Baronie d'Alluye (Eure-et-Loir), Le Fargis (Yvelines), Piennes (Somme), and Paris.
2 CONT * Jeanne de Piennes (d’Halluin), her surviving lineage and dower claims actually split into two primary branches: the Babou de La Bourdaisière / Sagonne line (from Robertet/Alluye) and the Angennes du Fargis line (from d'Angennes/
2 CONC Le Fargis).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''III. Narrative Biography: Court Matriarch and National Precedent'''
2 CONT Born in the 1530s into the main stem of Halluin-Piennes, Jeanne d'Halluin was appointed fille d'honneur to Catherine de' Medici upon her arrival in France. Growing up at the center of royal politics under the court moniker "La Pet
2 CONC ite Pienne," Jeanne served as a trusted court figure and visual staple of Valois elegance, immortalized in fine chalk drawings by François Clouet and portraiture by Corneille de Lyon.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the early 1550s, Jeanne exchanged a secret, binding promise of marriage (verba de futuro) with François de Montmorency, eldest son of Constable Anne de Montmorency. Under 16th-century canon law, mutual consent constituted an in
2 CONC dissoluble sacrament without parental approval. The union posed a severe threat to royal dynastic planning, as King Henri II had arranged for François to marry his legitimized daughter, Diane de France. Jeanne was removed from cou
2 CONC rt and confined to the Convent of the Filles-Dieu in Paris while the Crown petitioned Pope Paul IV. The betrothal was formally dissolved by papal and royal decree between 1556 and 1557. This scandal directly prompted Henri II's Ed
2 CONC ict of March 1556/57 against Clandestine Marriages, landmark legislation requiring parental consent for valid marriages.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the dissolution, a rapid marriage was arranged in 1557 between Jeanne and Florimond II/III Robertet, Baron d'Alluye, one of the Crown's chief Secrétaires d'État. This alliance rehabilitated Jeanne's court standing and an
2 CONC chored her family within the royal administrative apparatus. Following Robertet's death in 1569, Jeanne married royal diplomat Philippe d'Angennes, Seigneur du Fargis, expanding her network into ambassadorial circles until her dea
2 CONC th in 1589.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''IV. Heraldry & Visual Identity'''
2 CONT Armorial Bearings: Alliance shields impaling Halluin (paternal) with Robertet (1st marriage) and d'Angennes (2nd marriage).
2 CONT Blazons:
2 CONT Halluin: D'argent, à trois lions de sable, armés, lampassés et couronnés d'or.
2 CONT Robertet (1st Husband): D'azur, au chevron d'or accompagné de trois étoiles du même, au chef d'argent chargé de trois molettes de sable.
2 CONT d'Angennes (2nd Husband): De sable, à la croix de Saint-André d'argent.
2 CONT Iconography: Preserved in chalk drawings and oil portraiture by François Clouet (Musée Condé, Chantilly) inscribed JEANNE DE HALLUIN DAME D'ALLUYE.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''V. Primary Sources & Evidence Ledger'''
2 CONT * Archives Nationales (Paris) — Registres du Parlement de Paris (X/1a 1584, fol. 112):
2 CONT Royal Edict of Henri II (March 1556/57) against Clandestine Marriages (Édit contre les mariages clandestins), the primary statutory precedent born directly from the Jeanne d'Halluin–Montmorency crisis.
2 CONT Vatican Apostolic Archives (Rome) — Regestica Vaticana (Acta Pauli IV) / BnF MS Fr. 3158:
2 CONT Official papal bull and sentence of canonical release (1556/57) dissolving the unsolemnized matrimonial vow exchanged between François de Montmorency and Jeanne d'Halluin.
2 CONT * Archives Nationales (Paris) — Minutier Central des Notaires (Étude XIX):
2 CONT Notarized marriage contract (May 1557) between Florimond II/III Robertet, Baron d'Alluye, and Jeanne d'Halluin, detailing dower assignments and her filiation from Antoine Ier d'Halluin.
2 CONT * Bibliothèque Nationale de France (Paris) — Cabinet des Titres (Pièces Originales, vol. 84 - d'Angennes):
2 CONT Notarized marriage contract (c. 1570) between Messire Philippe d'Angennes, Seigneur du Fargis, and Dame Jeanne d'Halluin, widow of the Baron d'Alluye.
2 CONT * Musée Condé (Chantilly) & BnF Collection Clairambault (Vol. 814 - Comptes de la Maison de la Reine):
2 CONT Royal household payroll records (1550–1568) and Clouet workshop portrait studies confirming Jeanne's rank as Fille d'honneur and Dame d'honneur to Catherine de' Medici.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''VI. Project Guardrails & Profile Advisory'''
2 CONT '''Maternal & Generational Alignment:'''
2 CONT This profile records (Gen 15) Jeanne d'Halluin as the daughter of Antoine Ier d'Halluin x Louise de Crèvecœur and sister of Charles I (1st Duc), Jean (Sr. de Maintenay), and Louise (Dame de Brouilly), correcting older secondary li
2 CONC stings that misplaced her into Generation 16 or confused her mother with Louise de Croÿ.
2 CONT '''Onomastic & Ghost Profile Purge:'''
2 CONT Consolidates her sequential marriages to Florimond II/III Robertet and Philippe d'Angennes. Do NOT create duplicate Generation 16 entries under misattributed styles (e.g., Jeanne d'Halluin d'Angennes or de Toignel) or confuse her 
2 CONC with her 15th-century aunt, Jeanne d'Halluin (wife of Jean de Crèvecœur).
2 CONT <span style="color:red; font-weight:bold;">DISCLAIMER: This profile is generated with AI assistance as an analytical research framework. Large language models can hallucinate dates, confuse collateral branches, or make errors in t
2 CONC ranscriptions. All archival citations, chartulary receipts, and genealogical claims contained herein require verification against original primary sources before formal publication or certification. AI can make mistakes.</span>
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
