1 CONC aren ohne bleibenden Erfolg.Nachkommen  Herzog Stephan III. heiratete am 13. Oktober 1364 in Mailand die Prinzessin Taddea Visconti (1350–1381), Tochter des Visconte Bernabò Visconti und dessen Gattin Prinzessin Beatrix della Scal
1 CONC a. Aus der Ehe gingen zwei Kinder hervor:Ludwig VII. der Gebartete (1368–1447) Gift 1) 1402 in Prag Prinzessin Anna de la Marche (1380–1408), Witwe des Grafen Johann II. von Berry-Montpensier Gift 2) 1413 in Paris Prinzessin Katha
1 CONC rina von Alençon (1395–1462), Witwe des Grafen Peter von Evreux Elisabeth (Isabeau de Bavière, 1371–1435) Gift 1) 1385 in Amiens König Karl VI. von Frankreich (1368–1422) In zweiter Ehe heiratete er am 17. Januar 1401 in Köln Prin
1 CONC zessin Elisabeth von Kleve. Die Ehe blieb kinderlos.
0 @N6000000002188309239@ NOTE Louis VII, Duke of Bavaria
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Duke Louis VII of Bavaria (20 December 1365 – 1 May 1447, Burghausen) (German: Ludwig VII der Bärtige, Herzog von Bayern), was from 1413 Duke of Bavaria-Ingolstadt. He was a son of Stephen III.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Biography
1 CONT
1 CONT As brother of Isabella of Bavaria-Ingolstadt, wife of Charles VI of France, he spent several years in France, when he succeeded his father in 1413 he ordered to build the New Castle of Ingolstadt, which was strongly influenced by 
1 CONC French Gothic. In 1408 on the field of Othée Louis, William II, Duke of Bavaria-Straubing and John the Fearless defeated the citizens of Liege who revolted against William's brother John of Bavaria, the bishop of Liège.
1 CONT
1 CONT Very hot-tempered Louis was not only in conflict with his former ally John of Burgundy but fought also several times against his cousin Henry XVI of Bavaria-Landshut who had united his enemies in the Parakeet Society of 1414 and t
1 CONC he League of Constance of 1415.
1 CONT
1 CONT The death of John of Bavaria in 1425 caused a new conflict between Louis and his cousins Henry, Ernest, Duke of Bavaria-Munich and William III, Duke of Bavaria-Munich. As a result John's duchy Bavaria-Straubing then was partitione
1 CONC d between the four dukes in 1429.
1 CONT
1 CONT Finally, Louis was imprisoned in 1443 by his own son, Louis VIII, who had allied with Henry XVI. Louis died in 1447 as Henry’s prisoner. Since Louis VIII had died already two years before, the duchy of Bavaria-Ingolstadt passed to
1 CONC  Henry.
1 CONT Louis VII, Duke of Bavaria
1 CONT House of Wittelsbach
1 CONT Born: 20 December 1365 Died: 1 May 1447
1 CONT Regnal titles
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Stephen III Duke of Bavaria-Ingolstadt
1 CONT 1413–1443 Succeeded by
1 CONT Louis VIII
0 @N6000000000337154832@ NOTE (Research):Isabella of Bavaria  Encyclopædia Britannica Article   born 1371 died September 1435, Paris   Isabella also rendered Elizabeth , French Isabeau , or Élisabeth, de Bavière queen consort of Charles VI of France, who frequ
1 CONC ently was regent because of her husband's periodic insanity. Her gravest political act was the signing of the Treaty of Troyes (May 21, 1420), which recognized King Henry V of England as heir to the French crown in place of her so
1 CONC n Charles (afterward Charles VII), who was to be exiled from France.   The daughter of Stephen III, duke of Bavaria-Ingolstadt, she was married to Charles VI on July 17, 1385. Her husband's first severe attack of insanity (Aug. 5,
1 CONC  1392) caused her great distress; for years she sought remedies, both medical and supernatural. She bore the King six children between 1393 and 1403, but, as his illness grew worse, his rebuffs (he occasionally did not recognize h
1 CONC er) drove her into flagrant sexual misconduct. Her brother-in-law, Louis, duc d'Orléans, became her constant companion, though it has not been proved that he was her lover. After the murder of Orléans (1407) she relied on John the
1 CONC  Fearless, duke of Burgundy, who rescued her from imprisonment by her son, the dauphin Charles (1417), and with her set up governments, at Chartres and then at Troyes, that rivalled the administration at Paris.   Capricious and po
1 CONC litically unskilled, Isabella saw John the Fearless desert her with the intention of joining the dauphin Charles against the English, who were preparing to attack Paris. She died despised by both the French and the English.
0 @N6000000002322286616@ NOTE After her husband became insane, she consorted with duc d' Orleans until his
1 CONT death in 1407.
