0 @N6000000002187742881@ NOTE James I of Scotland
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT James I (December 10, 1394 – February 21, 1437) reigned as king of Scotland from April 4, 1406 until February 21, 1437. However, from 1406 to 1424 he was king in name only.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was born on December 10, 1394, the son of Robert III and Annabella Drummond. He had an eventful childhood. In 1402 his elder brother, David, was starved to death in prison at Falkland in Fife. Before the death of his father in 
1 CONC 1406 James was sent to France for safety.
1 CONT
1 CONT On the way there, the English captured him and handed him over to Henry IV of England who imprisoned him and demanded a ransom. Robert III was said to have died from grief over the capture of James. His uncle, Robert Stewart, 1st 
1 CONC Duke of Albany who became Regent on the death of Robert III, was in no hurry to pay for his release. Robert secured the release of his son Murdoch, who was captured at the same time, but not so with James. So for the next 18 years
1 CONC , James was imprisoned and educated in Windsor Castle and in secure large country houses near London.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the death of his uncle in 1420, the ransom of £40,000 was finally paid, and in 1424 James returned to Scotland to find a country in chaos. He took his bride with him – he had met and fallen in love with Joan Beaufort while i
1 CONC mprisoned. He married her in London in February 2, 1423. They would have eight children, including the future James II of Scotland, and Margaret, wife of Louis XI of France. He is believed during his captivity to have written The 
1 CONC Kingis Quair, an allegorical romance and one of the earliest major works of Scottish literature extent.
1 CONT
1 CONT James was formally crowned King of Scotland at Scone Abbey, Perthshire, on May 2 or 21, 1424. He immediately took strong actions to regain authority and control. One such action was to execute the Albany family, who had opposed hi
1 CONC s actions. The execution of Murdoch, Duke of Albany, and two of Murdoch's sons took place on May 24, 1425 at Castle Hill, Stirling.
1 CONT
1 CONT He proceeded to rule Scotland with a firm hand, and achieved numerous financial and legal reforms. For instance, for the purpose of trade with other nations, foreign exchange could only be exchanged within Scottish borders. He als
1 CONC o tried to remodel the Parliament of Scotland along English lines. However, in foreign policy, he renewed the Auld Alliance, a Scottish-French (and therefore anti-English) alliance, in 1428.
1 CONT
1 CONT His actions throughout his reign, though effective, upset many people. During the later years of his reign, they helped to lead to his claim to the throne coming under question.
1 CONT
1 CONT James I's grandfather, Robert II, had married twice and the awkward circumstances of the first marriage, from which James was descended, led to it being disputed. Conflict broke out between the descendants of the first marriage an
1 CONC d the unquestionably legitimate descendants of the second marriage over who should be on the Scottish throne. Matters came to a head on February 21, 1437, when James was assassinated by a group of Scots led by Sir Robert Graham wh
1 CONC ile staying at the Friars Preachers Monastery in Perth. He attempted to escape his assailants through a sewer. However, three days previously, he had the other end of the drain blocked up because it was connected to the tennis cou
1 CONC rt outside, and balls were in the habit of getting lost in it. (See also: Catherine Douglas.)
1 CONT
1 CONT A wave of executions followed in March, 1437, of those who were part of the plot. Among those executed were James's uncle, Walter, Earl of Atholl, and his grandson, Robert, Master of Atholl (both of whom were descended from Robert
1 CONC  II's second marriage).
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT See also
1 CONT Scottish monarchs family tree 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Reference
1 CONT Peter Wordie and Lance St John Butler (1989). "Tennis in Scotland" in The Royal Game. Stirling: Falkland Palace Real Tennis Club. ISBN 0-9514622-0-2 or ISBN 0-9514622-1-0. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Robert III King of Scots Succeeded by:
1 CONT James II 
0 @N6000000001783571644@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT Born on December 10, 1394, the son of Robert III and Annabella Drummond, he had an eventful childhood. In 1402 his elder brother, David, starved to death in prison at Falkland in Fife.
1 CONT
1 CONT Attempted Escape From Scotland
1 CONT
1 CONT Before the death of his father in 1406 the authorities sent James to France for safety. During his journey to France, the English captured the young prince and handed him over to Henry IV of England, who imprisoned him and demande
1 CONC d a ransom. Robert III allegedly died from grief over the capture of James. James's uncle, Robert Stewart, 1st Duke of Albany, who became Regent on the death of Robert III, showed no haste in paying for his nephew's release. Alban
1 CONC y secured the release of his own son Murdoch, captured at the Battle of Homildon Hill, but not so with James. So for the next 18 years James remained a prisoner/hostage in England. Henry IV had the young Scots King imprisoned and 
1 CONC educated in Windsor Castle and in secure large country houses near London.
1 CONT
1 CONT Scholars believe that during his captivity James wrote The Kingis Quair, an allegorical romance, one of the earliest major works of Scottish literature.
1 CONT
1 CONT Return To Scotland
1 CONT
1 CONT After the death of James's uncle in 1420, the Scots finally paid the ransom of £40,000, and in 1424 James returned to Scotland to find a country in chaos. He took his bride with him - he had met and fallen in love with Joan Beaufo
1 CONC rt, a cousin of King Henry VI of England, while imprisoned. He married her in London in February 2, 1423.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT James I and Joan Beaufort married and had the following children:
1 CONT Margaret of Scotland (1424-1445), wife of Louis XI of France.
1 CONT Isabella Stewart, Princess of Scotland 1426-1494 married Francis I, Duke of Brittany
1 CONT Eleanor Stewart, Princess of Scotland 1427-1480 married Sigismund, Archduke of Austria
1 CONT Mary of Scotland, Countess of Buchan 1428-1465 married Wolfart VI van Borsselen
1 CONT Joan of Scotland, Countess of Morton ca 1428-/1486 married James Douglas, 1st Earl of Morton
1 CONT Annabella of Scotland married and divorced 1. Louis of Savoy, and then married and divorced 2. George Gordon, 2nd Earl of Huntly.
1 CONT
1 CONT Death
1 CONT
1 CONT James I's grandfather, Robert II, had married twice and the awkward circumstances of the first marriage (the one with James's grandmother Elizabeth Mure) led some to dispute its validity. Conflict broke out between the descendants
1 CONC  of the first marriage and the unquestionably legitimate descendants of the second marriage over who had the better right to the Scottish throne. Matters came to a head on February 21, 1437, when a group of Scots led by Sir Robert
1 CONC  Graham assassinated James at the Friars Preachers Monastery in Perth. He attempted to escape his assailants through a sewer. However, three days previously, he had had the other end of the drain blocked up because of its connecti
1 CONC on to the tennis court outside, balls habitually got lost in it.
1 CONT
1 CONT A wave of executions followed, of those who had participated in the plot, in March 1437. The authorities executed (among others) James's uncle, Walter Stewart, 1st Earl of Atholl, and Atholl's grandson, Robert Stewart, Master of A
1 CONC tholl - both of them descended from Robert II's second marriage).
1 CONT
1 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_I_of_Scotland
