1 CONT The Infanta Isabel (pron. IPA: [iz?b??]) (February 21, 1397 – December 17, 1471) was a Portuguese infanta of the House of Aviz, the only surviving daughter of king John I of Portugal and his wife Philippa of Lancaster. She was the
1 CONC  sister, amongst others, of Henry the Navigator, Pedro, Duke of Coimbra and king Edward of Portugal. She was the third wife of Philip the Good, Duke of Burgundy, in right of which she was Duchess consort of Burgundy; her son by Ph
1 CONC ilip was Charles the Bold.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Early life
1 CONT     * 2 Duchess of Burgundy
1 CONT     * 3 Ancestors
1 CONT     * 4 Further Reading
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Early life
1 CONT
1 CONT Isabel was born in Évora and spent her youth in the Portuguese court in Lisbon. The only surviving daughter amongst five sons, she was brought up according to her mother's strict notions of etiquette and formality, but also indulg
1 CONC ed and protected. She, like her brothers, was given a good education by her parents, who desired their children to be not only healthy but intelligent: the Infanta was thus taught several languages, given a good grounding in mathe
1 CONC matics, and allowed to experiment in the sciences. Her father, the romantic but sensible John I, ensured that she was given a good understanding of politics, allowing her to share with her brothers their instructions in affairs of
1 CONC  state; her mother, the conservative and pragmatic Philippa, demonstrated an example of commitment to duty, firm discipline, and religious faith that would later prove very important to Isabel, as well as instilling in the Portugu
1 CONC ese infanta a favourable view of England based as much on pragmatic recognition of the advantages to any nation allied with that kingdom as on sentiment. With her brothers she would ride and hunt, and she became skilled in Latin, 
1 CONC French, English and Italian through her studies with the princes.
1 CONT
1 CONT Two events of importance happened to Isabel in 1415. The first was an offer of marriage by her cousin, Henry V of England, who desired to form closer links between England and Portugal against France. The marriage negotiations led
1 CONC  nowhere; Isabel, despite being, at 18 years old, already slightly old to be a first time bride, was apparently not particularly disappointed. She would not receive another offer for another 13 years. More importantly for her, in 
1 CONC that year, came the death of her mother, Philippa, who died on 19 June 1415. Isabel, who had been very close to her mother, briefly withdrew from court life in grief, taking refuge in her chambers with her ladies in waiting, and p
1 CONC racticing her skills in needlework and singing.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Duke of Burgundy, Philip the Good, had already been married twice by 1428: first to his second cousin, Michele of Valois, who had, like many of her family, gone mad, being prompted into a cycle of melancholy by the murder of h
1 CONC er father-in-law, John the Fearless, and secondly to Bonne of Artois, widow of his uncle, who had died in 1425, less than a year after the marriage. Neither marriage leaving surviving issue, his second widowhood had left him looki
1 CONC ng for a third wife, preferably from England, or a nation allied to England (his first two wives had been French; now, however, he was firmly allied with England, and wanted to secure that alliance with a suitable marriage). By 14
1 CONC 28, Isabel – despite being, at age 31, far beyond the common age of marriage for women – appeared attractive to him as a potential consort: shrewd, proud and skilful, she was perfectly capable of both maintaining diplomatic silenc
1 CONC es and giving good advice; herself healthy, her mother had borne six surviving children and came from a similarly fertile family; and she possessed the emotional, intellectual and ideological strength necessary to benefit any futu
1 CONC re husband's governance. Her country was allied to England; not only that, but the merchants of Flanders and Portugal enjoyed profitable trading that he wished to reinforce.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip thus sent a delegation, led by his chief counsellor, the Seigneur de Roubaix, from Sluys on 19 October 1428 which, after calling at Sandwich until 2 December and acquiring two more ships, arrived at Lisbon on 16 December. T
1 CONC he delegation waited a further month, whilst Isabel's father and brothers met at Aviz to discuss the matter; on 19 January, a formal request for the Infanta's hand was made by the Burgundians, and discussions between the two parti
1 CONC es began. The Portuguese agreeing to the marriage, messengers were sent on 2 February 1429 to receive the Duke of Burgundy's formal response, which was signed on 5 May and received by the Portuguese on 4 June. After further minor 
1 CONC matters, the marriage contract was drawn up, and Isabel, still in Portugal, was married to Philip the Good by proxy – Roubaix acting as groom – on 24 July 1429.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Duchess of Burgundy
1 CONT
1 CONT Despite her marriage, the new Duchess did not immediately leave Portugal for another 8 weeks; rather, whilst the weather swung between sun and storms, and her father had a fleet and trousseau prepared for his daughter, she enjoyed
1 CONC  a continued period of feasts, tournaments, plays, and festivals, all put on by the court and the people in mourning for the loss of their only Infanta, and happiness at her future as Duchess of Burgundy. On 19 October 1429, with 
1 CONC a flotilla of around 20 ships prepared, Isabel – accompanied by almost 2000 Portuguese – left Portugal forever. After a rough journey of eleven weeks, which saw the loss of several ships and much of her bridal trousseau, the convo
1 CONC y reached Sluys on 25 December 1429, the Duchess disembarking the following day (having been forced through illness and apprehension to rest for the remainder of Christmas Day). She and Philip celebrated their formal religious mar
1 CONC riage two weeks later, on 7 January, and the marriage was consummated shortly afterwards.
1 CONT
1 CONT Accompanied by her husband, and by the Countess of Namur, Jeanne de Harcourt, Isabel then travelled through the main territories of Burgundy: from Ghent (16 January) to Kortrijk (13 February) to Lille, and then to Brussels, Arras,
1 CONC  Peronne, Mechelen and, by mid-March Noyon, where Isabel, now pregnant, chose to rest through that Spring, only leaving when Joan of Arc led a campaign against the nearby Compiegne. For several months afterwards, the Duchess was f
1 CONC orced to deal with the rebellions and unrest of her husband's subjects and neighbours, as well as with administrative and financial issues, in all of which she proved equal to the task.
1 CONT
1 CONT What Isabel was not, at first, equal to was the style and complexity of court life in Burgundy. Although the Portuguese court had been by no means austere in her time, it had not matched up to the heights of fashion and flamboyanc
1 CONC e seen in Philip's court, one of the richest and most extravagant in Europe; by contrast, the conservatively brought up Portuguese Infanta, described by the Burgundian embassy that had negotiated her marriage as appearing to their
1 CONC  eyes as a nun when they had first met, and now dressed in loose clothing and flat over-panels to hide her pregnancy, looked particularly dowdy. More upsetting to Isabel, however, was her husband's behaviour. He had showered gifts
1 CONC  on her when she had first arrived, and still more when she had become pregnant; yet, he made it clear that he did not keep, had never kept, and had no intention of keeping, his vows of fidelity and chastity. In contrast to the st
1 CONC yle typical of nobles and royalty at that time – to keep a succession of honoured favourites or mistresses at their court or home – Philip had no particular mistress with whom to embarrass his wife. Instead, he kept numerous women
1 CONC  as his lovers, several at a time, most living away from the court, who would periodically present him with illegitimate children, of whom he had a great deal. In addition to these, he would flirt with the ladies of the court, on 
1 CONC occasion placing one on the pummel of his saddle as he rode in ceremonial progresses. His infidelities would give Isabel a certain amount of grief during their marriage.
1 CONT
1 CONT Isabel gave birth to her first child on 30 December 1430 at Coudenburg, a year after her marriage. The child, Anthony (in French, Antoine), was worryingly puny: tiny at birth, he had a weak cry and a listless appetite, none of whi
1 CONC ch boded well for the child. However, his Christening took place on 16 January 1431, and soon after both parents were once again attending to ducal business. By the autumn of that year, Isabel was once again pregnant with their se
1 CONC cond son Joseph; more importantly, she had spent a long continuous period of time with her husband, and demonstrated her intelligence and abilities, as well as her commitment to Burgundian independence. Because of this, when Charl
1 CONC es VII of France began attacking Burgundy in January 1432, Philip – leaving Coudenburg to defend Dijon – ordered, "You will serve the Duchess in her state and office representing me during my absence". This was obeyed, and Isabel 
1 CONC found herself treated with all the respect and deference she might expect as the Duchess of Burgundy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Antoine and Joseph both died in 1432, but the Duchess then gave birth to the future Charles the Bold on November 10, 1433.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Ancestors
1 CONT Infanta Isabel's ancestors in three generations Infanta Isabel,
1 CONT Duchess of Burgundy Father:
1 CONT John I of Portugal Father's father:
1 CONT Peter I of Portugal Father's father's father:
1 CONT Afonso IV of Portugal
1 CONT Father's father's mother:
1 CONT Beatrice of Castile
1 CONT Father's mother:
1 CONT Teresa Lourenço Father's mother's father:
1 CONT Lourenço Martins
1 CONT Father's mother's mother:
1 CONT Sancha Martins
1 CONT Mother:
1 CONT Philippa of Lancaster Mother's father:
1 CONT John of Gaunt, 1st Duke of Lancaster Mother's father's father:
1 CONT Edward III of England
1 CONT Mother's father's mother:
1 CONT Philippa of Hainault
1 CONT Mother's mother:
1 CONT Blanche of Lancaster Mother's mother's father:
1 CONT Henry of Grosmont, 1st Duke of Lancaster
1 CONT Mother's mother's mother:
1 CONT Isabel de Beaumont
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Isabel was a very refined and intelligent woman, who liked to be surrounded by artists and poets. She funded numerous scholarships and was a patron of the arts. Also in politics, she had a great influence on her son, but especiall
1 CONC y on her husband, whom she represented on several diplomatic conferences.By 1457 however, she had withdrawn from court as well as her husband, partly siding with her son in his estrangement with his father, partly due to a desire 
1 CONC to live a more devout and quieter life. She died in Dijon in 1471.
1 CONT
