1 CONT Charles V (21 January 1338 – 16 September 1380), called the Wise, was King of France from 1364 to his death and a member of the House of Valois. His reign marked a high point for France during the Hundred Years' War, with his armi
1 CONC es recovering much of the territory ceded to England at the Treaty of Brétigny.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Early life
1 CONT     * 2 The Regency and the uprising of the Third Estate
1 CONT     * 3 The Treaty of Bretigny
1 CONT     * 4 King of France
1 CONT     * 5 Le Sage
1 CONT     * 6 The war resumes
1 CONT     * 7 Papal Schism
1 CONT     * 8 Death
1 CONT     * 9 Legacy
1 CONT     * 10 Ancestors
1 CONT     * 11 Marriage and Issue
1 CONT     * 12 Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Early life
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles was born in Vincennes, Île-de-France, France, the son of King Jean II and Bonne of Luxembourg. Upon his father's succession to the throne in 1350, Charles became Dauphin of France. He was the first French heir to use the t
1 CONC itle, which is named for the region of Dauphiné, acquired by Charles' grandfather.
1 CONT
1 CONT The future king was highly intelligent but physically weak, with pale skin and a thin, ill-proportioned body. He made a sharp contrast to his father — who was tall, strong and sandy-haired — and gossip at the time suggested he was
1 CONC  not Jean's son. Similar rumors would pursue Charles' grandson, Charles VII.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] The Regency and the uprising of the Third Estate
1 CONT
1 CONT King Jean was a brave warrior but a poor ruler who alienated his nobles through arbitrary justice and the elevation of associates considered unworthy. After a three-year break, the war resumed in 1355, with Edward, The Black Princ
1 CONC e, leading an English-Gascon army in a violent raid across southwestern France. After checking an English incursion into Normandy, Jean led an army of about 16,000 south, crossing the Loire in September, 1356, attempting to outfla
1 CONC nk the Prince's 8,000 soldiers at Poitiers. Rejecting advice from one captain to surround and starve the Prince — a tactic Edward feared — Jean attacked the strong enemy position. In the subsequent Battle of Maupertuis (Poitiers),
1 CONC  English archery all but annihilated the French cavalry, and Jean was captured. Charles led a battalion at Poitiers which withdrew early in the struggle; whether the order came from Jean (as he later claimed) or whether Charles hi
1 CONC mself ordered the withdrawal is unclear.
1 CONT French Monarchy
