0 @N6000000005076720078@ NOTE Louis II de Mâle, Comte de Flandre was born on 25 November 1330.1 He was the son of Louis I de Flandre, Comte de Flandre and Marguerite d' Artois, Comtesse d'Artois.2 He married Marguerite de Brabant, daughter of Jean III de Braba
1 CONC nt, Duc de Brabant and Marie d'Evreux, on 1 July 1347.2,1 He died in 1384.1
1 CONT      Louis II de Mâle, Comte de Flandre gained the title of Comte de Flandre in 1346.3
1 CONT Child of Louis II de Mâle, Comte de Flandre and Marguerite de Brabant
1 CONT Marguerite de Flandre, Comtesse de Flandre+ b. 13 Apr 1350, d. 16 Mar 14053
1 CONT Citations
1 CONT [S45] Marcellus Donald R. von Redlich, Pedigrees of Some of the Emperor Charlemagne's Descendants, volume I (1941; reprint, Baltimore, Maryland, U.S.A.: Genealogical Publishing Company, 2002), page 57. Hereinafter cited as Pedigre
1 CONC es of Emperor Charlemagne, I.
1 CONT [S16] Jirí Louda and Michael MacLagan, Lines of Succession: Heraldry of the Royal Families of Europe, 2nd edition (London, U.K.: Little, Brown and Company, 1999), table 74. Hereinafter cited as Lines of Succession.
1 CONT [S16] Louda and MacLagan, Lines of Succession, table 65.
0 @N6000000002187845357@ NOTE Louis II of Flanders
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis II of Flanders (October 25, 1330, Male – January 30, 1384, Lille), known as Louis of Male, was the son of Louis I of Flanders and Marguerite of France, and Count of Flanders.
1 CONT
1 CONT On his father's death at the Battle of Crécy in 1346, he inherited the counties of Flanders, Nevers, and Rethel. In 1347, he married Margaret of Brabant (1323–1368), daughter of John III, Duke of Brabant. They had three children:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Pierre (d. March 3, 1376)
1 CONT     * Charles (predeceased his father)
1 CONT     * Margaret III of Flanders (1348–1405)
1 CONT
1 CONT He also left several illegitimate sons, three of whom were killed at the Battle of Nicopolis.
1 CONT
1 CONT On the death of his father-in-law in 1355, he took the title of Duke of Brabant, but was unable to wrest the duchy from his sister-in-law Joanna, Duchess of Brabant.
1 CONT
1 CONT The latter years of his rule were marked by civil strife. In 1379, he obtained aid from his son-in-law, Philip II of Burgundy, to put down a revolt in Ghent. The Flemings again rose under Philip van Artevelde and expelled him from
1 CONC  Flanders after the Battle of Beverhoutsveld; however, the influence of Philip procured a French army to relieve him, and the Flemings were decisively defeated at the Battle of Roosebeke. However, the citizens of Ghent continued t
1 CONC o resist (with English aid) until after his death in 1384. His mother had died two years previously, leaving him the counties of Artois and the Franche-Comte.
1 CONT
1 CONT As he left no surviving sons, on his death, his extensive possessions in the Low Countries were inherited by his daughter Margaret.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Louis I Count of Flanders, Nevers and Rethel
1 CONT 1346–1384 Succeeded by
0 @N6000000002322303501@ NOTE Her husband ordered the notorious murder of his cousin Louis, duc d' Orleans
1 CONT in 1407 by hired assassins to subdue Paris. Louis's followers were a faction
1 CONT called the Armagnacs, who drove the Burgundians out of Paris (1413-1318). In
1 CONT the autumn of 1419, John, in hope of making a peace settlement with the
1 CONT Armagnacs, arranged a meeting with the leader, dauphin Charles, in an
1 CONT alliance against England. They met on a bridge, each with ten companions. As
1 CONT the parley began, John was killed, almost certainly carefully premeditated.
