0 @N6000000002187911974@ NOTE James Douglas, 9th Earl of Douglas
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT James Douglas, 9th Earl of Douglas, 3rd Earl of Avondale (1426–1488), was a Scottish nobleman, last of the 'Black' earls of Douglas.
1 CONT
1 CONT He succeeded to the earldom on the murder of his brother William Douglas, 8th Earl of Douglas by KingJames II and his entourage. He denounced his brother's murderers and took up arms against the king, but was forced to give in whe
1 CONC n his allies deserted him.
1 CONT
1 CONT He obtained a papal dispensation to marry his brother's widow, Margaret Douglas, the Fair Maid of Galloway, in order to keep the family estates together. He was involved in intrigues with the English court, and in 1455 rebelled ag
1 CONC ainst James II once more.
1 CONT
1 CONT Meanwhile another branch of the Douglas family, known as the Red Douglases, had risen into importance, and George Douglas, 4th Earl of Angus, great-grandson of the first earl of Douglas, took sides with the king against the Earl o
1 CONC f Douglas. Douglas, again deserted by his chief allies, fled to England, and his three younger brothers, Ormond, Moray and Balvany, were defeated by Angus at the battle of Arkinholm, near Langholm on the Esk. Moray was killed, Orm
1 CONC ond taken prisoner and executed, and Balvany escaped to England. Their last stronghold, Threave Castle in Galloway, fell. James Douglas was attainted in 1455, and his lands and estates were forfeit to the crown. The lands of the D
1 CONC ouglases were divided among their rivals, the lordship of Douglas falling to the Red Douglas 4th earl of Angus.
1 CONT
1 CONT In England the earl of Douglas continued to intrigue against his native land; he was employed by Edward IV in 1461 to negotiate a league with the western highlanders against the Scottish kingdom. He married again after this time, 
1 CONC to Anne Holland, daughter of John Holland, 1st Duke of Exeter, his previous marriage having ended in divorce following his attainder.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1484 he was taken prisoner while raiding southern Scotland, and was relegated to the abbey of Lindores, where he died in 1488.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT William Douglas Earl of Douglas
1 CONT 1452–1455 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Forfeit
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT William Douglas Earl of Avondale
1 CONT 1452–1455 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Forfeit
