0 @N6000000002188559685@ NOTE Louis of Sicily
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis the Child (1337 – 16 October 1355) was king of Sicily, then called Trinacria, from 1342 to 1355.
1 CONT
1 CONT His father was Peter II, whom he succeeded at the age of five, and his mother was Elisabeth of Carinthia. His reign began under the joint regency of his uncle Giovanni, the duke of Randazzo, and his mother.
1 CONT
1 CONT He lived at Randazzo until 1347. In that year, the plague called the Black Death arrived. In 1348, the duke named Blasco II de Alagona, a Catalan, regent. Rivalry—before then mitigated by the duke—between the Italian noblesse (the
1 CONC  families of Chiaramonte, Palizzi, and Scaloro degli Uberti; called the filoangioini) and the Catalan noblesse (the families of Peralta, Alagona, and Ventimiglia; called the filoaragonesi) degenerated into civil war. Louis, a Cata
1 CONC lan by descent, sent the royal army after the Chiaramonte unto Milazzo. Only in 1350 did peace arrive.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1355, the king's cousin Frederick, lord of Aci and son of the duke of Randazzo, died. In an attempt to avoid the plague, Louis left the fortress Agira for the castle of Aci. He died in the Acese fortress on 16 October in that y
1 CONC ear, of the plague, and only seventeen years of age. He was buried in the cathedral of Catania next to Frederick II (III) and Giovanni, the old regent.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Peter II King of Sicily
1 CONT 1342–1355 Succeeded by
1 CONT Frederick III
0 @N6000000002188378352@ NOTE Frederick III the Simple
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick III or IV (1 September 1341 – 27 January 1377), called the Simple, King of Sicily from 1355 to 1377, was the second son of Peter II of Sicily and Elisabeth of Carinthia. He succeeded his brother Louis. The documents of h
1 CONC is era call him the "infante Frederick, ruler of the kingdom of Sicily," without any regnal number.
1 CONT
1 CONT "Frederick the Simple" is often confused with an earlier Sicilian monarch, Frederick II, who chose to call himself "Frederick III", even though he was actually only the second King Frederick to occupy the Sicilian throne, as also 
1 CONC this Fredrick has been dubbed by later generations of genealogists and historians as Frederick III.
1 CONT
1 CONT The beginning of Frederick III's reign was plagued by intermittent wars with the Kingdom of Naples and also by the Black Death, to which his elder brother and predecessor had succumbed. In 1372 he was able to come to peace terms w
1 CONC ith Naples and the Papacy and was titled as a Tributary King of "Trinacria."
1 CONT
1 CONT He appointed his uncle, Roland, to act on his behalf in the Duchy of Athens and Neopatria.
1 CONT
1 CONT On 11 April 1361, Frederick married his first wife Constança of Aragon, daughter of Peter IV of Aragon. They had only one daughter, Mary. On 17 January 1372, Frederick married secondly Antonia of Balzo. There were no children from
1 CONC  this marriage.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Louis King of Sicily
1 CONT 1355–1377 Succeeded by
1 CONT Mary
1 CONT Duke of Athens
1 CONT 1355–1377
0 @N6000000006127668377@ NOTE Rudolf II. der Blinde (* 8. August 1306 in Wolfratshausen; † 4. Oktober 1353 in Neustadt) war Pfalzgraf bei Rhein von 1329 bis 1353.Er war der Sohn Pfalzgraf Rudolfs I. († 1319) und der Mechthild von Nassau († 1323), Tochter König
1 CONC  Adolfs von Nassau. Sein Onkel war Kaiser Ludwig IV. der Bayer († 1347).Seine Kindheit war geprägt vom innerfamiliären Kampf zwischen Vater und Onkel. Nach dem Tod seines Vaters stand er mit seiner Mutter und seinen Brüdern Adolf 
1 CONC († 1327) und Ruprecht unter der Vormundschaft des Grafen Johann von Nassau, der einAnhänger der österreichischen Partei war. Ludwig IV. der Bayer hatte auf Grund des mit Rudolf I. am 26. Februar 1317 abgeschlossenen Vertrages unte
1 CONC r Waffengewalt Besitz von der Rheinpfalz genommen. Im August 1322 fand der Krieg ein Ende, aber erstnach Mechthilds Tod im Juni 1323, deren Hass gegen Ludwig IV. eine dauernde Versöhnung unmöglich machte, kam es zwischen dem König
1 CONC  und den drei Neffen zu einer Annäherung. Im Jahre 1328 wurde eine Landesteilung vollzogen, die am 4. August 1329 imHausvertrag von Pavia bestätigt wurde. Ludwig der Bayer und dessen Söhne erhielten Oberbayern und den Rest der Obe
1 CONC rpfalz. Zusammen mit seinem jüngeren Bruder Ruprecht übernahm Rudolf die Regierung in der Rheinpfalz. Am 18. Februar 1338 kam es zueiner weiteren Teilung mit Ruprecht und seinem Neffen Ruprecht II., wobei er u. a. Neustadt mit der
1 CONC  Burg Winzingen erhielt. Nach dem Tod Kaiser Ludwigs wählte Rudolf zusammen mit seinem Bruder im Jahre 1349 Günther von Schwarzburg zum Gegenkönig.Dennoch verlobte er kurz darauf seine Tochter Anna mit dessen Rivalen Karl IV..Ein 
1 CONC Augenleiden führte zur namensgebenden Blindheit in seinem letzten Lebensjahr. Er wurde in der St.-Aegidius-Kirche in Neustadt an der Hardt bestattet.
0 @N6000000002322312740@ NOTE After her marriage to John Henry, Prince of Luxembourg, she expelled John
1 CONT Henry in November 1341; whereupon Louis, declaring that the marriage had not
1 CONT been consumated and was therefore void, married her with ill-considered haste
1 CONT to his widowed son, Louis of Brandenberg, on February 10, 1342. This created
1 CONT unfavourable impression throughout the empire. A new election was held and
1 CONT Charles of Moravia, who granted excessive concessions to the Pope, was elected
1 CONT new German king, July 1346. Louis died of a heart attack in 1347 bear hunting.
0 @N6000000002322312724@ NOTE This marriage to Louis IV led to the accession of Holland & its dependencies.
