0 @N6000000002188309476@ NOTE Margaret of Anjou
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT jeanne (talk) 16:13, 3 April 2008 (UTC)
1 CONT Margaret of Anjou
1 CONT Queen consort of England
1 CONT Margaret of Anjou with King Henry VI
1 CONT Born 23 March 1430
1 CONT Duchy of Lorraine
1 CONT Died 25 August 1482, 63
1 CONT Anjou
1 CONT Buried Angers Cathedral
1 CONT Consort 23 April 1445 – 21 May 1471
1 CONT Consort to Henry VI
1 CONT Issue Edward, Prince of Wales
1 CONT Royal House House of Valois
1 CONT House of Lancaster
1 CONT Father René I of Naples
1 CONT Mother Isabella, Duchess of Lorraine
1 CONT
1 CONT Margaret of Anjou (Marguerite d'Anjou, 23 March 1430 – 25 August 1482) was the Queen consort of Henry VI of England from 1445 to 1471, and led the Lancastrian contingent, in the Wars of the Roses.
1 CONT
1 CONT Margaret was born in the duchy of Lorraine, an Imperial fief east of France that was ruled by the cadet branch of the French kings, the house of Anjou. Margaret was the daughter of René I of Naples, Duke of Anjou, King of Naples a
1 CONC nd Sicily and Isabella, Duchess of Lorraine in her own right. Margaret married King Henry VI, who was eight years her senior, on 23 April 1445, at Titchfield in Hampshire.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry, who had more interest in religion and learning than in military matters, was not a successful king. He had reigned since he was a few months old and his actions had been controlled by regents. When he married Margaret, his 
1 CONC mental condition was already unstable, and by the time their only son, Edward of Westminster, was born, on 13 October 1453, he had suffered a complete mental breakdown. Rumours were rife that he was incapable of fathering a child 
1 CONC and that the new Prince of Wales was the result of an adulterous liaison on Margaret's part.
1 CONT
1 CONT Margaret seems to have been quite mild-mannered until her husband was threatened with deposition by the ambitious Richard, Duke of York. With the king captured, Margaret managed to escape, and immediately began raising an army in 
1 CONC Wales and the north of England, where she was assisted by Henry's half-brother, Jasper Tudor. In 1459, hostilities resumed at the battle of Blore Heath, where Margaret is said to have witnessed her commander, James Touchet, Lord A
1 CONC udley defeated by a Yorkist army under Richard Neville, Earl of Salisbury.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT While she was attempting to raise further support for the Lancastrian cause in Scotland[1], her principal commander, Henry Beaufort, Third Duke of Somerset[2], gained a major victory for her at the Battle of Wakefield on 30 Decemb
1 CONC er 1460, by defeating the combined armies of Richard Plantagenet, Duke of York, and the Earl of Salisbury. Margaret had both beheaded, and ordered their heads displayed on the gates of the city of York. She followed up with a vict
1 CONC ory at St Albans on 22 February 1461, at which she defeated the Yorkist forces of Richard Neville, Earl of Warwick, and recaptured her husband.
1 CONT
1 CONT On 4 March 1461, the Lancastrian army was beaten at the Battle of Towton by the son of the late Duke of York, Edward IV of England, who deposed King Henry and proclaimed himself king. Margaret was determined to win back her son's 
1 CONC inheritance, and fled with him into Wales and later Scotland. Finding her way to France, she made an ally of King Louis XI of France, and at his instigation she allowed an approach from Edward's former supporter, Richard Neville, 
1 CONC Earl of Warwick, who had fallen out with his former friend and was now seeking revenge for the loss of his political influence. Warwick's daughter, Anne Neville, was married to Margaret's son, Edward, Prince of Wales, in order to 
1 CONC cement the alliance, and Margaret insisted that Warwick return to England to prove himself, before she followed. He did so, restoring Henry VI briefly to the throne on 3 October 1470.
1 CONT
1 CONT By the time Margaret, her son and daughter-in-law were ready to follow Warwick back to England, however, he had been defeated and killed by the returning King Edward IV in the Battle of Barnet on 14 April 1471, and Margaret was fo
1 CONC rced to lead her own army at the Battle of Tewkesbury on 4 May 1471, at which they were defeated and her son was killed. Over the previous ten years, she had gained a reputation for aggression and ruthlessness, but now she was a b
1 CONC roken spirit, imprisoned at both Wallingford Castle and in the Tower of London until ransomed by the French king in 1475. She died on 25 August 1482, in Anjou, where she was buried.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Further reading
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Maurer, Helen E. Margaret of Anjou: Queenship and Power in Late Medieval England. Boydell Press, 2003.
1 CONT
1 CONT Margaret of Anjou
1 CONT House of Valois
