0 @N6000000002187780999@ NOTE Frederick I of Denmark
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick I
1 CONT King of Denmark and Norway
1 CONT King Frederick I
1 CONT Born October 7, 1471
1 CONT Died April 10, 1533
1 CONT Buried Roskilde Cathedral
1 CONT Predecessor Christian II
1 CONT Successor Christian III
1 CONT Consort Anna of Brandenburg
1 CONT Sophie of Pomerania
1 CONT Father Christian I
1 CONT Mother Dorothea of Brandenburg
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick I of Denmark and Norway (October 7, 1471 – April 10, 1533) was the son of the first Oldenburg King Christian I of Denmark, Norway and Sweden (1426–1481) and of Dorothea of Brandenburg (1430–1495). The name is also spelle
1 CONC d Friedrich in German, Frederik in Danish, and Fredrik in Swedish and Norwegian.
1 CONT
1 CONT The underage Frederick was elected co-Duke of Schleswig and Holstein in 1482, soon after the death of his father, the other co-duke being his ten years elder brother Johann (or Hans), the King. At Frederick's majority in 1490 both
1 CONC  duchies were divided between the brothers.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1500 he'd convinced his brother and co-duke for a conquest of Dithmarschen, and a great army was called from not only the duchies, but with additions from all of the Kalmar Union for which his brother briefly was king. Also num
1 CONC erous German mercenaries took part. The expedition failed however miserably in the Battle of Hemmingstedt, where one third of all knights of Schleswig and Holstein lost their lives.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1523 his nephew Christian II, the King of Denmark and Sweden, was forced by disloyal nobles to abdicate, and the duke took the throne as king Frederick I. A group of Jutish nobles had offered Frederick the throne as early as 15
1 CONC 13, when his brother king Hans died, but he had declined, rightly believing that the majority of the Danish nobility would be loyal to prince Christian.
1 CONT
1 CONT During his rule as a Danish king Frederick had to suppress social revolts among the peasants at the same time as the rise of the Protestant movement made a balancing attitude a necessity. Without being a man of greater statesmansh
1 CONC ip Frederick managed to escape all open conflicts even though he seems to have accepted the spread of Lutheran propaganda. 1532 he succeeded in capturing Christian II who had tried to get a political come-back in Norway.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family and children
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1502 he married Anna of Brandenburg (15 years old; a daughter of the cousin of his mother; 1487–1514). The couple had two children:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Christian, the future duke and king (12 August 1503 – 1 January 1559)
1 CONT    2. Dorothea (1 August 1504 – 11 April 1547), married 1 July 1526 to Duke Albert of Prussia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick's wife Anna died on May 5, 1514, 26 years old.
1 CONT
1 CONT Four years later, Frederick married Sophie of Pomerania (20 years old; 1498–1568), a daughter of Duke Bogislaw "the Great" of Pomerania. Sophie and Frederick had six children:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Duke John of Holstein (28 June 1521 – 2 October 1580)
1 CONT    2. Elizabeth (14 October 1524 – 15 October 1586), married:
1 CONT          1. on 26 August 1543 to Duke Magnus III of Mecklenburg-Schwerin
1 CONT          2. on 14 February 1556 to Duke Ulrich III of Mecklenburg-Güstrow
1 CONT    3. Duke Adolf of Holstein-Gottorp (25 January 1526 – 1 October 1586)
1 CONT    4. Anna (1527 – 4 June 1535)
1 CONT    5. Dorothea (1528 – 11 November 1575), married on 27 October 1573 to Duke Christof of Mecklenburg-Schwerin.
1 CONT    6. Bishop Friedrich of Hildesheim and Schleswig (13 April 1532 – 27 October 1556).
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1525 his son, the future king Christian III, married Dorothea of Saxe-Lauenburg (14 years old; 1511–1571).
1 CONT
1 CONT He died on April 10, 1533 in Gottorp, at an age of 61.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Christian II King of Denmark
1 CONT 1523–1533 Succeeded by
1 CONT Christian III
1 CONT King of Norway
1 CONT 1523–1533
0 @N6000000006127680728@ NOTE Fredrik I, hertig av Holstein, greve av Oldenburg. kung av Danmark år 1523-år 1533, Född 1471, son till Christian I av Oldenburg och Danmark, och Dorotea av Brandenburg. Död 1533. Han kröntes den 7 augusti 1524.Fredrik stödde till
1 CONC sammans med den Lübeckska hansan Gustav Vasa i hans uppror mot Fredriks brorsonen, Christian II. Syftet var att manövrera bort kungen och själv få danska tronen, något som lyckades.Barn utanför äktenskap med Hilleke Hansdotter Deg
1 CONC ener.Katerine Fredriksdotter, född 1491, död 1534 Geseke Fredriksdotter, född 1493, död 1535 i Husum, Schleswig-Holstein. Gift med 1) Anna av Brandenburg Hoenzollern.Barn:Kristian III av Danmark född 1503, död 1559. Dorotea av Dan
1 CONC mark född 1504, död 1547. Gift med 2) Sophie av Pommern.Barn:Hans d.ä. av Oldenburg (i Danmark Johan d.ä.) död barnlös 1580. Adolf av Holstein-Gottorp, hertig av Holstein-Gottorp, född 1526 (stamfader för -> Holstein-Gottorpska ät
1 CONC ten) 
0 @N6000000007329564283@ NOTE Mit håb er Gud alene.         Konge af Danmark-Norge 1523 -33   Fr. var
1 CONT opdraget i hertugdømmerne, og blev på forældrenes foranledning valgt til
1 CONT 'landsfader'   Kong Hans' lov om rigets udelelighed stoppede det., Men
1 CONT det halve af hertugdømmerne fik han d   stedet blev det nevøen Chr. II.
1 CONT Da denne i 1523 flygtede blev han igen tilbudt kronen, og den   have
1 CONT forrang frem for Fr.'s. Dette medførte store forsvarsudgifter. Fr. var
1 CONT hele livet igenne katolske og den spirende protestantiske lære, det
1 CONT sidste i strid med håndfæstningen, men hvad
0 @N6000000002187780989@ NOTE Hans of Denmark
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT King of Denmark, Sweden, Norway, the Wends and the Goths, Duke of Schleswig, Holstein, Stormarn and Dithmarschen, Count of Oldenburg and Delmenhorst[1] (more...)
1 CONT King of Norway (more...)
1 CONT Reign 1483 – July 22, 1513
1 CONT Predecessor Christian I
1 CONT Successor Christian II
1 CONT Coronation 20 July 1483
1 CONT King of Denmark (more...)
1 CONT Reign 1481 – July 22, 1513
1 CONT Predecessor Christian I
1 CONT Successor Christian II
1 CONT Coronation 18 May 1483
1 CONT King of Sweden (more...)
1 CONT Reign 1497 – 1501
1 CONT Predecessor Steen Sture the Old (regent)
1 CONT Successor Steen Sture the Old (regent)
1 CONT Coronation November 26, 1497
1 CONT Consort Christina of Saxony
1 CONT Issue
1 CONT Christian
1 CONT Elizabeth of Denmark
1 CONT Royal house House of Oldenburg
1 CONT Father Christian I
1 CONT Mother Dorothea of Brandenburg
1 CONT Born February 2, 1455(1455-02-02)
1 CONT Aalborg Castle
1 CONT Died February 20, 1513 (aged 58)
1 CONT Aalborg Castle
1 CONT Burial Odense
1 CONT
1 CONT Hans (Danish, Norwegian and Swedish) (2 February 1455 – 20 February 1513), was union king of Denmark (1481 – 1513), Norway (1483 – 1513) Sweden (1497 – 1501), under the Kalmar Union, and also Duke of Schleswig and Holstein.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was born on February 2, 1455 as the son of Christian I and Dorothea of Brandenburg, daughter of Margrave Hans of Brandenburg. In 1478 he married Christina of Saxony, granddaughter of Frederick the Gentle of Saxony. This produce
1 CONC d the following offspring: Christian II, Franciscus, Knud, and Elisabeth, who later married as princess of Brandenburg. He died on February 20, 1513.
1 CONT
1 CONT The three most important political goals of King Hans seem to have been the recovery of the union, fight against the Hanseatic League and the making of a strong Danish royal power. He worked to obtain all three things during his r
1 CONC eign.
1 CONT
1 CONT Hans' father, king Christian I, in 1458 had the Norwegian Council of the Realm commit to electing Christian's eldest son as next king of Norway upon his death. A similar declaration was made in Sweden. In 1467, Hans was hailed as 
1 CONC successor to the throne in Denmark. Hans used the title heir to the throne of Norway, in line with Norway's old status as a hereditary kingdom, but this was a claim the Norwegian Council did not immediately recognise. Consequently
1 CONC , upon king Christian's death in May 1481, Hans' position was unchallenged in Denmark, whereas in Norway the Council of the Realm assumed royal authority, and an interregnum ensued. No serious rival candidates to the Norwegian thr
1 CONC one existed, but the Council was determined on demonstrating Norway's status as a sovereign kingdom. A meeting between the Councils of Denmark, Sweden and Norway was appointed for 13 January 1483 at Halmstad, to work out the terms
1 CONC  for electing Hans as king - his håndfæstning. The Swedish Council failed to turn up at the meeting, but the Norwegian and Danish councils proceeded to produce a joint declaration containing the terms for Hans' rule, and electing 
1 CONC him king. It was hoped that Sweden would later accept the same document, and also acknowledge Hans as king. Subsequently, Hans was crowned king of Denmark in Copenhagen on 18 May, and king of Norway in Trondheim on 20 July.
1 CONT
1 CONT During the first years of his rule he carried on a balancing policy. By diplomatic means he tried to weaken the position of the Swedish regent Sten Sture, and he also sought new allies – he was the first Danish king to have establ
1 CONC ished a political co-operation with Russia. After the 1493 treaty, Ivan III of Russia imprisoned all Hanseatic merchants trading in Novgorod and instigated the Russo-Swedish War, 1496-1499. The Hanseatic cities were also troubled 
1 CONC by a secret war by Danish privateers (a more modest Danish forerunner of the policy of Queen Elizabeth of England against Spain). At that time the position of the Hansabund was also slowly but steadily declining because of the cha
1 CONC nges in trade routes and the growing opposition against the Hanseatic League in the Northern European naval states.
1 CONT
1 CONT Hans' domestic politics were marked by an economic support of the Danish merchants and by a widespread use of commoners as officials and even as councillors, something which angered the nobility. The most important of his initiati
1 CONC ves was perhaps establishing a permanent Danish navy, one which came to play a role during his later years.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the Privilege of Ribe the Noble Diets of the duchies of Schleswig and Holstein were to elect a duke among the sons of the previous duke. Many nobles in Schleswig and Holstein preferred Hans' much younger brother Frede
1 CONC rick, but Hans successfully argued for electing both the late king's sons as co-dukes. Although it was initially agreed they should govern the duchies jointly, at the majority of Frederick (in 1490) the duchies were nevertheless d
1 CONC ivided.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1497 Hans conquered Sweden during a short and effective military campaign after in advance having undermined the position of Sten Sture by winning most of the Swedish nobility. After the victory the king acted wisely and pardon
1 CONC ed his enemies.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1500 he made a fatal attempt at conquering the Ditmarshes (Dithmarschen) in Northern Germany, an area which the kings of Denmark had long viewed as belonging to their realm, but which was in reality an independent peasant repub
1 CONC lic. Together with his brother Frederick he carried through en large-scale campaign based upon an army of German mercenaries, but the Ditmarshers caught most of the army in a trap after having opened the dykes of the low-land area
1 CONC  and flooded the narrow main road.
1 CONT
1 CONT The defeat hurt Hans’s prestige, and in 1501 Sweden renounced him as king. Hans fought an increasingly more bitter war against Sten Sture and his successor Svante Nilsson, and this conflict meant frictions with both the Danish nob
1 CONC ility and the Hanseatic cities, especially Lübeck. In 1509, with the Netherlands acting as arbiters, Sweden agreed to a declaration which recognised Hans as king of Sweden in principle, but he was never allowed into Stockholm as l
1 CONC ong as he lived, nor crowned king of Sweden anew.
1 CONT
1 CONT Meanwhile, Norwegian attempts at opposition were strangled by Hans’s son prince Christian (afterwards King Christian II) who was the viceroy of Norway from 1507.
1 CONT
1 CONT 1510-12 the king fought a last war with both Sweden and Lübeck in which Denmark was at first very pressed but partly turned the tables by a naval offensive. The result concerning Sweden was the status quo; in return Lübeck was suf
1 CONC fering a real political and economic setback by the peace.
1 CONT
1 CONT In his own age, and partly to posterity, Hans has often appeared a “commoner’s king”, a jolly and plain man with a folksy manner. Behind the surface, however, he seems to have been a hard realist and a zealous political calculator
1 CONC . In many ways he is a Scandinavian parallel of Louis XI of France and Henry VII of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT After his son was deposed in 1522, Hans' blood returned to the Danish and Norwegian thrones in the person of Christian IV of Denmark, the great-great-grandson of his daughter, Electress Elisabeth. He is thought to have been the fa
1 CONC ther of the Franciscan missionary to New Spain Jacob Dacian.
1 CONT Hans of Denmark
1 CONT House of Oldenburg
1 CONT Born: February 2 1455 Died: July 22 1513
