1 CONT
1 CONT Facts about this person:
1 CONT
1 CONT Fact 1October 13, 1399
1 CONT Acceded: Westminster Abbey, London, England
1 CONT
1 CONT Fact 2
1 CONT Interred: Canaterbury Cathedral, Canterbury, Kent
0 @N6000000001563226808@ NOTE Henry IV, King of England, ruled from 1399 to 1413. We was the son of John of Gaunt and Blanche of Lancaster. 
1 CONT
1 CONT King Henry IV was the first of the 3 kings of the House of Lancaster.  It was claimed that he was not a very attractive character.  He was a solid looking man with a good head for business. He knew how to win a throne by striking 
1 CONC down a feeble adversary and he knew how to keep it by making tactful concessions to the right people. The crown which he won was no joy to him; Shakespeare's "Uneasy lies the head that wears a crown" was applied, most rightly to H
1 CONC enry IV.
1 CONT
1 CONT His reign was divided into 2 halves.  During the first the king was harassed by rebellions; during the second by constant ill health and the ambitions of his heir.  These 2 divisions can be studied in dramatic form in the two part
1 CONC s of William Shakespeare's King Henry the Fourth.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry's reign was punctuated by repeated rebellions in the North and the Glyndwr rebellion in the West. Despite this he successfully held the Crown and passed it on to his son, Henry V, though their relationship was always uneasy.
1 CONC  his reign effectively ended all military use of castles in Brecknock.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry IV was born at Bolingbroke in 1367 to John of Gaunt and Blanche of Lancaster. He married Mary Bohun in 1380, who bore him seven children before her death in 1394. In 1402, Henry remarried, taking as his bride Joan of Navarre
1 CONC . 
1 CONT Henry had an on-again, off-again relationship with his cousin, Richard II . He was one of the Lords Appellant who, in 1388, persecuted many of Richard's advisor-favorites, but his excellence as a soldier gained the king's favor - 
1 CONC Henry was created Duke of Hereford in 1397. In 1398, however, the increasingly suspicious Richard banished him fov ten years. John of Gaunt's death in 1399 prompted Richard to confiscate the vast Lancastrian estates; Henry invaded
1 CONC  England while Richard was on campaign in Ireland, usurping the throne from the king. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The very nature of Henry's usurpation dictated the circumstances of his reign - incessant rebellion became the order of the day. Richard's supporters immediately revolted upon his deposition in 1400. In Wales , Owen Glendower led 
1 CONC a national uprising that lasted until 1408; the Scots waged continual warfare throughout the reign; the powerful families of Percy and Mortimer (the latter possessing a stronger claim to the throne than Henry) revolted from 1403 t
1 CONC o 1408; and Richard Scrope, Archbishop of York, proclaimed his opposition to the Lancastrian claim in 1405. 
1 CONT Two political blunders in the latter years of his reign diminished Henry's support. His marriage to Joan of Navarre (of whom it was rumored practiced necromancy) was highly unpopular - she was, in fact, convicted of witchcraft in 
1 CONC 1419. Scrope and Thomas Mawbray were executed in 1405 after conspiring against Henry; the Archbishop's execution alarmed the English people, adding to his unpopularity. He developed a nasty skin disorder and epilepsy, persuading m
1 CONC any that God was punishing the king for executing an archbishop. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Crushing the myriad of rebellions was costly, which involved calling Parliament to fund such activities. The House of Commons used the opportunity to expand its powers in 1401, securing recognition of freedom of debate and freedom
1 CONC  from arrest for dissenting opinions. Lollardy, the Protestant movement founded by John Wycliffe during the reign of Edward III , gained momentum and frightened both secular and clerical landowners, inspiring the first anti-heresy
1 CONC  statute, De Heritico Comburendo, to become law in 1401. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry, ailing from leprosy and epilepsy, watched as Prince Henry controlled the government for the last two years of his reign. In 1413, Henry died in the Jerusalem Chamber of Westminster Abbey. Rafael Holinshed explained his unpo
1 CONC pularity in Chronicles of England : "... by punishing such as moved with disdain to see him usurp the crown, did at sundry times rebel against him, he won(himself more hatred, than in all his life time ... had been possible for hi
1 CONC m to have weeded out and removed." Unlikely as it may seem (due to the amount of rebellion in his reign), Henry left his eldest son an undisputed succession.
