0 @N6000000004270567756@ NOTE http://www.landersgen.com/landers/62/7283.htm
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert II Stewart, King of Scotland
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Born: 2 Mar 1316, Paisley, Renfrewshire, Scotland 631,766,787
1 CONT     * Marriage (1): Elizabeth Mure on 22 Nov 1347 787
1 CONT     * Marriage (2): Countess Euphemia of Ross after 2 May 1355 766
1 CONT     * Died: 19 Apr 1390, Dundonald Castle, Ayrshire, Scotland at age 74 631,766,787
1 CONT     * Buried: 1390, Scone Island, Perthshire, Scotland 766
1 CONT
1 CONT   General Notes:
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert II (1316-90), king of Scotland from 1371 to his death. He became the founder of the Stewart dynasty. The most notable imcidents of Robert's reign were the invasion of Scotland by an English military and naval force under th
1 CONC e command of the Duke of Lancaster in 1384, and again by King Richard II in 1385, which wasted the land as far as Edinburgh and Fife, and the grand retaliatory expedition of the Scotch in 1388. [The Home University Encyclopedia, 1
1 CONC 946]
1 CONT
1 CONT Notes on Robert II, King of Scotland
1 CONT Appointed the 6th High Steward of Scotland on 9 Apr 1326 and created Earl of Atholl 16 Feb 1341. Throughout the disastrous reign of his uncle, David II, he was one of the most prominent nobles of Scotland. He twice acted as Regent
1 CONC  (1335-1341 and 1346-1357). Upon David's death he obtained the crown, becoming the founder of the powerful Stewart dynasty. His powerful and intractable barons shaped the policy of the country very much according to their own plea
1 CONC sure. The misery inflicted by their raids into and across the border marches was frightful. The reprisals by the English wardens were equally painful. The great events of his reign were invasions of Scotland by an English force un
1 CONC der the Duke of Lancaster in 1384 and by Richard II in 1385 and the retaliatory expedition of the Scots in 1388 culminating in the defeat at Otterburn. Robert first married his mistress by (papal?) dispensation, legitimising his n
1 CONC ine children by her; and secondly by papal dispensation after the death of his first wife. {Burke's Peerage and Chamber's Biographical Dictionary} [GADD.GED]
1 CONT
1 CONT   Noted events in his life were:
1 CONT
1 CONT  Title: High Steward of Scotland, 9 Apr 1326. (6th)
1 CONT
1 CONT  Title: Regent of Scotland, 1335-1341.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Title: Earl of Atholl, 16 Feb 1341.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Title: Regent of Scotland, 1346-1357.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Title: King of Scotland, 1371-1390.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert married Elizabeth Mure, daughter of Sir Adam Mure and Unknown, on 22 Nov 1347.787 (Elizabeth Mure died before 1355 631.)
1 CONT
1 CONT   Noted events in their marriage were:
1 CONT
1 CONT  Alt. Marriage: Alt. Marriage, 1336. 773
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert next married Countess Euphemia of Ross after 2 May 1355.766 (Countess Euphemia of Ross died in 1387.)
0 @N6000000004533918195@ NOTE Robert II, King of Scots; born 2 March 1315/6; succeeded father as7th Great Steward of Scotland 1327; fought at Battle of Halidon Hill1333; commanding the retreat after his uncle David II was captured atNeville's Cross 1346, Regen
1 CONC t or Guardian of Scotland 1346-7, succeededDavid II 1370/1; married 1st (papal dispensation 22 Nov 1347)Elizabeth, daughter of Sir Adam Muir of Rowallan; married 2nd (papaldispensation 2 May 1355) Eupheme (died 1387), daughter of 
1 CONC Hugh, 4thEarl of Ross, and widow of John, 3rd Earl of Moray (killed atNeville's Cross 1346), and died 19 April 1390.  [Burke's Peerage]
1 CONT
1 CONT --------------
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert II Stewart, b. 2 Mar 1315/6, d. Dundonald Castle 19 Apr 1390,Earl of Atholl 1341, Earl of Strathern 1358, King of Scotland 22 Feb1370/1 - 1390; m. (1) 22 Nov 1347 (Papal dispensation), Elizabeth,daughter of Sir Adam Mure, o
1 CONC f Rowallan, co. Ayr, and Joan Cunningham.[Magna Charta Sureties]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Robert II (March 2, 1316- April 19, 1390), king of Scotland, called"the Steward", a title that gave the name to the House of Stewart (orStuart).
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert was the sole son of Walter, the 6th High Steward of Scotland(d. 1326), and Marjorie (d. 1316 in childbirth), daughter of KingRobert the Bruce.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1318 the Scottish parliament decreed that if King Robert diedwithout sons the crown should pass to his grandson; but the birth of ason afterwards, King David II, to Bruce in 1324 postponed theaccession of Robert for nearly fort
1 CONC y-two years. Soon after the infantDavid became king in 1329, the Steward began to take a prominent partin the affairs of Scotland. He was one of the leaders of the Scottisharmy at the battle of Halidon Hill in July 1333; and after
1 CONC  gainingsome successes over the adherents of Edward Balliol in the west ofScotland, he and John Randolph, 3rd Earl of Moray (d. 1346), werechosen as regents of the kingdom, while David sought safety in France.
1 CONT
1 CONT The colleagues soon quarrelled; then Randolph fell into the hands ofthe English and Robert became sole regent, meeting with such successin his efforts to restore the royal authority that the king was ableto return to Scotland in 1
1 CONC 341. Having handed over the duties ofgovernment to David, the Steward escaped from the battle of Neville'sCross in 1346, and was again chosen regent while the king was acaptive in England. Soon after this event some friction arose
1 CONC  betweenRobert and his royal uncle. Accused, probably without truth, ofdesertion at Neville's Cross, the Steward as heir-presumptive wasgreatly chagrined by the king's proposal to make Edward III ofEngland, or one of his sons, the
1 CONC  heir to the Scottish throne, and byDavid's marriage with Margaret Logie.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1363 he rose in rebellion, and after having made his submission wasseized and imprisoned together with four of his sons, being onlyreleased a short time before David's death in February 1371. By theterms of the decree of 1318 R
1 CONC obert now succeeded to the throne, andwas crowned at Scone in March 1371. He was not a particularly activeking. Some steps were taken by the nobles to control the royalauthority. In 1378 a war broke out with England; but the king 
1 CONC took nopart in the fighting, which included the burning of Edinburgh and theScottish victory at the Battle of Otterbourne in 1388.
1 CONT
1 CONT As age and infirmity were telling upon him, the estates in 1389appointed his second surviving son Robert, Earl of Fife, afterwardsDuke of Albany, guardian of the kingdom. The king died at Dundonald in1390, and was buried at Scone.
1 CONT
1 CONT His first wife was Elizabeth, daughter of Sir Adam Mure of Rowallan, alady who had formerly been his mistress. Robert had married her in1336, but as the marriage had been criticised as uncanonical, heremarried her in 1349. By her 
1 CONC he had at least four sons, includingAlexander of Buchan, Robert of Albany, his successor Robert III, andsix daughters. By his second wife, Euphemia, daughter of Hugh, 6thEarl of Ross, and widow of the 3rd Earl of Moray, formerly h
1 CONC iscolleague as regent, he had two sons and several daughters.
1 CONT
1 CONT The confusion about the circumstances of his first marriage wouldlater lead to conflict amongst the descendants of his first marriage(which included James I of Scotland) and the unquestionably legitimatedescendants of his second m
1 CONC arriage.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert had also eight illegitimate children, mostly by unknownmothers.
0 @N6000000001783571845@ NOTE Basic Life Facts
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert was the sole son of Walter Stewart, 6th High Steward of Scotland (d. 1326) and Marjorie Bruce, daughter of King Robert I of Scotland and his first wife Isabella of Mar. He was delivered by caesarean section, reputedly at Pa
1 CONC isley Abbey. His mother, who had been thrown from a horse, survived his birth by some hours at most.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1318 the Parliament of Scotland decreed that if King Robert died without sons the crown should pass to Marjorie's son; but the birth of a son afterwards, King David II, to Bruce in 1324 postponed the accession of Robert for nea
1 CONC rly forty-two years.
1 CONT
1 CONT Regent
1 CONT
1 CONT Soon after the infant David became king in 1329, the Steward began to take a prominent part in the affairs of Scotland. He was one of the leaders of the Scottish army at the Battle of Halidon Hill in July 1333; and after gaining s
1 CONC ome successes over the adherents of Edward Balliol in the west of Scotland, he and John Randolph, 3rd Earl of Moray (d. 1346), were chosen as regents of the kingdom, while David sought safety in France.
1 CONT
1 CONT The regents soon quarrelled; then Randolph fell into the hands of the English and Robert became sole regent, meeting with such success in his efforts to restore the royal authority that the king was able to return to Scotland in 1
1 CONC 341. Having handed over the duties of government to David, the Steward escaped from the Battle of Neville's Cross in 1346, and was again chosen regent while the king was a captive in England. Soon after this event some friction ar
1 CONC ose between Robert and his royal uncle. Accused, probably without truth, of desertion at Neville's Cross, the Steward as heir-presumptive was greatly chagrined by the king's proposal to make Edward III of England, or one of his so
1 CONC ns, the heir to the Scottish throne, and by David's marriage with Margaret Logie.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1363 he rose in rebellion, and after having made his submission was seized and imprisoned together with four of his sons, being only released a short time before David's death in February 1371. By the terms of the decree of 131
1 CONC 8 Robert now succeeded to the throne, and was crowned at Scone, Perthshire in March 1371. He was not a particularly active king. Some steps were taken by the nobles to control the royal authority. In 1378 a war broke out with Engl
1 CONC and; but the king took no part in the fighting, which included the burning of Edinburgh and the Scottish victory at the Battle of Otterburn in 1388.
1 CONT
1 CONT As age and infirmity were telling upon him, and in 1389 the Parliament appointed his second surviving son Robert, Earl of Fife, afterwards Duke of Albany, guardian of the kingdom.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriages and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT His first wife was Elizabeth Mure, by her he had at least ten children:
1 CONT King Robert III of Scotland (d. 1406), originally called John
1 CONT Robert Stewart, 1st Duke of Albany (1339-1420)
1 CONT Alexander Stewart, 1st Earl of Buchan, and Lord of Badenoch, "the Wolf of Badenoch" (1343-1405)
1 CONT Margaret Stewart, married John of Islay, Lord of the Isles
1 CONT Walter Stewart (d. 1362), married Isabella, Countess of Fife
1 CONT Marjory Stewart, married first John Dunbar, 5th Earl of Moray, second Alexander Keith
1 CONT Johanna Stewart, married in 1373 Sir John Keith, in 1379 Sir John Lyon, in 1384 Sir James Sandilands
1 CONT http://www.geo.ed.ac.uk/scotgaz/people/famousfirst1054.html
1 CONT Isabella Stewart, married first James Douglas, 2nd Earl of Douglas, married second David Edmondstone
1 CONT Katherine Stewart, married Sir Robert Logan of Grugar, later also of Restalrig, Lord High Admiral of Scotland
1 CONT Elizabeth Stewart, married Sir Thomas Hay, Lord High Constable of Scotland
1 CONT
1 CONT By his second wife, Euphemia de Ross, daughter of Aodh, Earl of Ross, and widow of the 3rd Earl of Moray, formerly his colleague as regent, he had five children:
1 CONT David Stewart, 1st Earl of Caithness (d. bef. 1389)
1 CONT Walter Stewart, 1st Earl of Atholl (d. 1437)
1 CONT Margaret Stewart
1 CONT Elizabeth Stewart, married 1380 David Lindsay, 1st Earl of Crawford
1 CONT Egidia Stewart, married 1387 Sir William Douglas of Nithsdale
1 CONT
1 CONT The confusion about the circumstances of his first marriage would later lead to conflict amongst the descendants of his first marriage (which included James I of Scotland) and the unquestionably legitimate descendants of his secon
1 CONC d marriage.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert had also eight illegitimate children: By Moira Leitch:
1 CONT John Stewart of Bute, Sheriff of Bute, "the Black Stewart"
1 CONT
1 CONT By Marion Cardny:
1 CONT Alexander Stewart, Canon of Glasgow
1 CONT John Stewart of Dundonald, "the Red Stewart"
1 CONT James Stewart of Kinfauns
1 CONT Walter Stewart
1 CONT John Stewart of Burley, Lord of Burley
1 CONT Sir John Stewart of Cairdney
1 CONT Sir Alexander Stewart of Inverlunan
1 CONT Thomas Stewart, Dean of Dunkeld, Archdeacon and Bishop-elect of St. Andrews
1 CONT
1 CONT Death
1 CONT
1 CONT The king died at Dundonald in 1390, and was buried at Scone.
1 CONT
1 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_II_of_Scotland
1 CONT
1 CONT Other Source
1 CONT
1 CONT Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert II Stewart, King of Scotland was born on 2 March 1316 at Paisley, Renfrewshire, Scotland. He was the son of Walter Stewart, 6th High Steward of Scotland and Margorie Bruce, Princess of Scotland. He married, firstly, Elizabe
1 CONC th Mure of Rowallan, daughter of Sir Adam Mure of Rowallan and Janet Mure, on 22 November 1347, by Papal dispensation, which legitimised their previously born children. He married, secondly, Eupheme de Ross, Countess of Moray, dau
1 CONC ghter of Hugh de Ross, 4th Earl of Ross and Margaret Graham, on 2 May 1355, by Papal dispensation.4 He died on 19 April 1390 at age 74 at Dundonald Castle, Ayrshire, Scotland. He was buried at Scone Abbey, Scone, Perthshire, Scotl
1 CONC and.5
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert II Stewart, King of Scotland succeeded to the title of 7th High Steward of Scotland on 9 April 1327. He fought in the Battle of Halidon Hill on 19 July 1333 at Halidon Hill, Scotland, where he was in command. He held the of
1 CONC fice of Regent of Scotland between 1338 and 1341. He was created 1st Earl of Atholl [Scotland] on 16 February 1341/42. He held the office of Regent of Scotland between 1346 and 1357. He was created 1st Earl of Strathearn [Scotland
1 CONC ] in 1358. He abdicated as Earl of Atholl on 31 May 1367. He abdicated as Earl of Strathearn on 18 April 1369. He gained the title of Earl of Strathearn on 4 April 1370. He succeeded to the title of King Robert II of Scotland on 2
1 CONC 2 February 1371. He was crowned King of Scotland on 26 March 1371 at Scone Abbey, Scone, Perthshire, Scotland.
1 CONT
1 CONT Before his accession, he had been successively joint and sole regent in David II's absence. During his reign, from 1384, his two sons were the real rulers for their unmilitary father.
1 CONT
