0 @N6000000002187788423@ NOTE James Douglas, 7th Earl of Douglas
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT James Douglas, 7th Earl of Douglas, 1st Earl of Avondale (1371–24 March 1443), known as 'the Gross' was a Scottish nobleman. He was the second son of Archibald Douglas, 3rd Earl of Douglas and Joan Moray.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1437 he was created Earl of Avondale. He succeeded to the earldom of Douglas on the summary execution of his great-nephew William Douglas, 6th Earl of Douglas, and his brother David, in 1440. James Douglas was himself implicate
1 CONC d in this attempt to neutralise the power of the 'Black' Douglas line.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage and issue
1 CONT
1 CONT He married first Beatrice Stewart, daughter of Robert Stewart, 1st Duke of Albany and Margaret Graham, Countess of Menteith. They had one daughter.
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Beatrice Douglas
1 CONT
1 CONT He married second Beatrice Sinclair, daughter of Henry Sinclair, 2nd Earl of Orkney, around 1425. They had five children.
1 CONT
1 CONT     * William Douglas (1425–1452), who succeeded his father
1 CONT     * James Douglas (1426–1491), who succeeded his brother as 9th earl
1 CONT     * Janet Douglas
1 CONT     * Margaret Douglas (d.1473)
1 CONT     * Beatrice Douglas
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT William Douglas Earl of Douglas
1 CONT 1440–1443 Succeeded by:
1 CONT William Douglas
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT New Creation Earl of Avondale
1 CONT 1437–1443 Succeeded by:
1 CONT William Douglas
